Object.assign(window.IND_FEED_BODY, {"ewd-mahabharata-31-0":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-31-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Last Day","text":"गदा","roman":"gada","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["mace","fruit","sea"],"a":0,"after":"गदा (gada) means “mace”."},"why":"A word from The Last Day","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"],["Hear it","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.dhola-maru-1":{"src":"story:fk.dhola-maru:scenes:1:text","title":"Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel · 2 of 9","body":"The families went home across the sands, the years went by, and in Narwar the promise gathered dust and was — not broken, exactly. Forgotten. Dhola grew up knowing nothing of Pugal. But in Pugal, Maru remembered. And Maru was not a person who let a true thing stay lost.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-dhola-maru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.dhola-maru|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.dhola-maru-2":{"src":"story:fk.dhola-maru:scenes:2:text","title":"Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel · 3 of 9","body":"Letters went astray, the ballad says, and messengers were turned back. So Maru sent the one thing the desert cannot stop: singers. Wandering minstrels who walked to Narwar and stood at Dhola's court and sang — sang of Pugal, of the old promise, of the princess who kept it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-dhola-maru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.dhola-maru|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.dhola-maru-3":{"src":"story:fk.dhola-maru:scenes:3:text","title":"Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel · 4 of 9","body":"The song struck Dhola like cold water. A promise — his promise — alive all these years in Pugal, kept by someone he had never met! He called for the swiftest camel in Narwar, and the ballad gives that camel almost as much love as the prince: a wind-drinker, a sand-skimmer, tireless as the horizon.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-dhola-maru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.dhola-maru|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.dhola-maru-5":{"src":"story:fk.dhola-maru:scenes:5:text","title":"Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel · 6 of 9","body":"They set out for Narwar together, and now the ballad becomes a proper chase. A desert chieftain, Umra, wished to stop them and sent riders after the pair; the singers stretch this part deliciously — hoofbeats behind, the camel running smooth as poured water, Maru navigating by the dunes she knew.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-dhola-maru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.dhola-maru|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.dhola-maru-6":{"src":"story:fk.dhola-maru:scenes:6:text","title":"Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel · 7 of 9","body":"The trouble of the road, the ballad insists, they beat together — each rescue answered by another, turn and turn about, until the pursuers' dust dropped below the horizon and the camel walked them into Narwar, thin, triumphant, and never once outrun.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-dhola-maru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.dhola-maru|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.dhola-maru-7":{"src":"story:fk.dhola-maru:scenes:7:text","title":"Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel · 8 of 9","body":"You can still see them today: on painted walls all over Rajasthan, two figures on one camel, mid-stride across somebody's doorway. Any child of the desert can tell you who they are. Dhola and Maru, going home. The camel, naturally, gets painted the biggest.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-dhola-maru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.dhola-maru|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Dhola, Maru and the Wind-Swift Camel","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.pabuji-camels-1":{"src":"story:fk.pabuji-camels:scenes:1:text","title":"How the Camels Came to the Desert · 2 of 8","body":"Pabuji, the epic tells, promised his little niece Kelam a wedding gift like no other: camels. She had never seen one. Hardly anyone in the desert had — for in those days, the tradition says, there were no camels in Rajasthan at all. They lived far away, beyond the desert's edge, guarded and not for sale.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pabuji-camels.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pabuji-camels|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How the Camels Came to the Desert","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pabuji-camels-2":{"src":"story:fk.pabuji-camels:scenes:2:text","title":"How the Camels Came to the Desert · 3 of 8","body":"The court smiled at the promise. Strange humped beasts from beyond the sands, for a little girl's wedding? But Pabuji had said it, and in the desert epics a spoken promise is iron.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pabuji-camels.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pabuji-camels|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How the Camels Came to the Desert","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pabuji-camels-3":{"src":"story:fk.pabuji-camels:scenes:3:text","title":"How the Camels Came to the Desert · 4 of 8","body":"The journey fills many verses — hard riding, cleverness, danger met and beaten — and the singers give it everything they have. And at the end of it, up out of the southern horizon into Rajasthan, for the first time ever in the epic's world, came the camels: tall, swaying, curious-eyed, complaining musically about everything, the way camels have done ever since.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pabuji-camels.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pabuji-camels|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How the Camels Came to the Desert","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pabuji-camels-5":{"src":"story:fk.pabuji-camels:scenes:5:text","title":"How the Camels Came to the Desert · 6 of 8","body":"And this is why the camel-herding people of the desert — the Raika, also called Rebari — hold Pabuji as their own special protector. The Raika say their community was entrusted with the care of camels from the beginning, and it is Pabuji they sing to when a camel falls sick. The herds and the hero belong to each other.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pabuji-camels.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pabuji-camels|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How the Camels Came to the Desert","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pabuji-camels-6":{"src":"story:fk.pabuji-camels:scenes:6:text","title":"How the Camels Came to the Desert · 7 of 8","body":"So when the bhopa reaches the camel verses, lamp held to the painted herd on the phad, the herders in the audience sit up straighter. That is not just a story on the scroll. That is the family trade, being handed its founding papers.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pabuji-camels.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pabuji-camels|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How the Camels Came to the Desert","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.deshnoke-mice-1":{"src":"story:fk.deshnoke-mice:scenes:1:text","title":"The Temple of the Small Ones · 2 of 8","body":"Step inside, and the temple's famous wonder is everywhere underfoot: mice. Thousands upon thousands of them — sleek, quick, entirely at home — drinking milk from great bowls, flowing along the walls, dozing in warm heaps. Here they are called kaba, and they are not pests. They are the whole point.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-deshnoke-mice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.deshnoke-mice|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Temple of the Small Ones","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.deshnoke-mice-2":{"src":"story:fk.deshnoke-mice:scenes:2:text","title":"The Temple of the Small Ones · 3 of 8","body":"The tradition tells it this way: Karni Mata, grieving for a child of her people, won from Yama, the lord of death, an extraordinary arrangement — her people would not pass into death's realm, but would live on as kaba in her temple, and be born back into her community again. The mice, in other words, are family.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-deshnoke-mice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.deshnoke-mice|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Temple of the Small Ones","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.deshnoke-mice-3":{"src":"story:fk.deshnoke-mice:scenes:3:text","title":"The Temple of the Small Ones · 4 of 8","body":"So watch the pilgrims' feet. Nobody strides through this temple. Everyone slides their feet along the floor, never lifting them, shuffling like skaters.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-deshnoke-mice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.deshnoke-mice|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Temple of the Small Ones","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.deshnoke-mice-5":{"src":"story:fk.deshnoke-mice:scenes:5:text","title":"The Temple of the Small Ones · 6 of 8","body":"And among the thousands of dark kaba live a few white ones — rarely seen, eagerly hoped for. To spot a white kaba, pilgrims say, is a special blessing: some hold they are Karni Mata and her closest kin themselves. Children search for them with the happy patience of birdwatchers.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-deshnoke-mice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.deshnoke-mice|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Temple of the Small Ones","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.deshnoke-mice-6":{"src":"story:fk.deshnoke-mice:scenes:6:text","title":"The Temple of the Small Ones · 7 of 8","body":"The Charans have served this temple for centuries, and Karni Mata's standing runs deep in Rajasthan — the rulers of Bikaner and Jodhpur honoured her in her lifetime, and her shrine has been cared for by her people ever since.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-deshnoke-mice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.deshnoke-mice|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Temple of the Small Ones","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chand-baori-1":{"src":"story:fk.chand-baori:scenes:1:text","title":"The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down · 2 of 8","body":"Stand at the rim and look down and your eyes slide on the pattern — steps, steps, steps, sharpening down into green shadow where the water waits at the bottom, cool as a cellar while the desert above bakes. It is more than a thousand years old, and it still takes the breath exactly as intended.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chand-baori.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chand-baori|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chand-baori-2":{"src":"story:fk.chand-baori:scenes:2:text","title":"The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down · 3 of 8","body":"The village tells its story the proper way. Chand Baori, they say, was built in a single night — by spirits. Between one sunset and one sunrise the ground opened, the thousands of steps laid themselves down in the dark, and the dawn found the well complete, too perfect for human hands and too deep for human patience.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chand-baori.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chand-baori|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chand-baori-3":{"src":"story:fk.chand-baori:scenes:3:text","title":"The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down · 4 of 8","body":"And the old records tell it the other way: the well was ordered by a king remembered as Chanda, of the Nikumbha line, more than a thousand years ago — cut and fitted by human builders, stone by stone, over years. So a child at the rim gets two stories and one excellent question.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chand-baori.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chand-baori|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chand-baori-5":{"src":"story:fk.chand-baori:scenes:5:text","title":"The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down · 6 of 8","body":"And a baori was never just plumbing. Down those stairs it is cool when the world above is furnace; the deep galleries were meeting place, resting place, a shaded refuge on pilgrim roads. The desert people made their water-place beautiful because they gathered at it — you decorate the room where everyone meets.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chand-baori.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chand-baori|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chand-baori-6":{"src":"story:fk.chand-baori:scenes:6:text","title":"The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down · 7 of 8","body":"Rajasthan and Gujarat are dotted with hundreds of stepwells — some plain, some carved like palaces — each one a signature of the same understanding: in dry country, water is the treasure, and the treasury deserves architecture. Chand Baori is simply the signature written largest.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chand-baori.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chand-baori|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Well That Goes Down Like a Mountain Upside-Down","facts":[["From","The Western Lands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.gond-mahua-1":{"src":"story:fk.gond-mahua:scenes:1:text","title":"The Tree That Feeds Everyone · 2 of 8","body":"Here is its wonder. In the hot season, when the fields are empty and food is shortest, the mahua flowers — and the flowers themselves are food. Fat, pale, honey-sweet little globes, and the tree does not wait to be asked: each night it simply lets them fall. By dawn the ground beneath is scattered with sweetness.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gond-mahua.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gond-mahua|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Feeds Everyone","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.gond-mahua-2":{"src":"story:fk.gond-mahua:scenes:2:text","title":"The Tree That Feeds Everyone · 3 of 8","body":"So in mahua season the villages rise before the sun. Whole families walk out to their trees — for the trees are known and kept, passed down like fields, each family gathering under its own — and pick the fallen flowers into baskets while the light comes up. Eaten fresh, dried for the year, cooked into sweets: the forest's own harvest, given without a plough.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gond-mahua.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gond-mahua|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Feeds Everyone","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.gond-mahua-3":{"src":"story:fk.gond-mahua:scenes:3:text","title":"The Tree That Feeds Everyone · 4 of 8","body":"A Gond elder, asked why the mahua alone is honoured so, put the question back, as elders do: \"Think of every way a tree can feed you. Now tell me — what does the mahua keep for itself?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gond-mahua.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gond-mahua|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Feeds Everyone","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.gond-mahua-5":{"src":"story:fk.gond-mahua:scenes:5:text","title":"The Tree That Feeds Everyone · 6 of 8","body":"The Gond honour the great god they call Bada Dev, and the old ways of the forest are kept in his sight: the first offerings, the songs at gathering time, the respect for the trees that stand between the people and hunger. The mahua stands first among those trees, and the tellings of how it came to the people differ from village to village — each one carrying the same thanks.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gond-mahua.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gond-mahua|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Feeds Everyone","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.gond-mahua-6":{"src":"story:fk.gond-mahua:scenes:6:text","title":"The Tree That Feeds Everyone · 7 of 8","body":"Watch the mahua work through a whole year: flowers in the hungry season, then fruit whose seed gives lamp-oil and cooking oil, then deep shade in the furnace months, then leaves for the cattle. An entire household economy, standing on one trunk, renewing itself for free.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gond-mahua.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gond-mahua|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Feeds Everyone","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.tansen-tigers-1":{"src":"story:it.tansen-tigers:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy Who Spoke Tiger · 2 of 8","body":"The boy, the accounts agree, had an ear like nobody else's. What he heard, he could give back. The creak of a cart, a peacock's cry, a herdsman's call across a valley — one hearing, and it was his. And his masterpiece, polished in the forests around Behat, was the roar of a tiger.","art":"art/story/sm/it-tansen-tigers.jpg","route":"#/story/it.tansen-tigers|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Spoke Tiger","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.tansen-tigers-2":{"src":"story:it.tansen-tigers:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy Who Spoke Tiger · 3 of 8","body":"The tradition tells of travellers on the forest road scattering for the trees at a roar from the thickets — and of the thicket then producing, instead of a tiger, one small boy, delighted beyond measure. The story is told laughing, and one suspects the boy told it best himself, later, to emperors.","art":"art/story/sm/it-tansen-tigers.jpg","route":"#/story/it.tansen-tigers|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Spoke Tiger","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.tansen-tigers-3":{"src":"story:it.tansen-tigers:scenes:3:text","title":"The Boy Who Spoke Tiger · 4 of 8","body":"One day — so the tradition continues — the great singer-saint Swami Haridas came along that road with his disciples, and the boy in the bushes gave his tiger everything he had. But a master of sound is a bad audience for a trick of sound. Haridas listened, and heard what the others had not.","art":"art/story/sm/it-tansen-tigers.jpg","route":"#/story/it.tansen-tigers|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Spoke Tiger","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.tansen-tigers-5":{"src":"story:it.tansen-tigers:scenes:5:text","title":"The Boy Who Spoke Tiger · 6 of 8","body":"From there the record firms up. Tansen's fame filled the courts of central India — first at Rewa, then summoned to the greatest court of all: Akbar's. The emperor made him one of the navaratnas, the nine jewels, and the chronicles of the age wrote of his singing as of a wonder of the world.","art":"art/story/sm/it-tansen-tigers.jpg","route":"#/story/it.tansen-tigers|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Spoke Tiger","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.tansen-tigers-6":{"src":"story:it.tansen-tigers:scenes:6:text","title":"The Boy Who Spoke Tiger · 7 of 8","body":"Around the man, stories gathered like moths — ragas that lit lamps, ragas that called rain. Those are their own kathas, told with love and badged as such wherever this app tells them. What is solid underneath is remarkable enough: the dhrupad tradition he carried, and generations of musicians tracing their lineage to him.","art":"art/story/sm/it-tansen-tigers.jpg","route":"#/story/it.tansen-tigers|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Spoke Tiger","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.teejan-pandavani-1":{"src":"story:it.teejan-pandavani:scenes:1:text","title":"The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata · 2 of 8","body":"The tambura is the secret weapon. In a Pandavani singer's hands it stops being an instrument and becomes everything else: swung up, it is Bhima's mace. Levelled, it is Arjuna's bow. Raised overhead, a chariot banner. The audience sees a battlefield; the prop list is one lute.","art":"art/story/sm/it-teejan-pandavani.jpg","route":"#/story/it.teejan-pandavani|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.teejan-pandavani-2":{"src":"story:it.teejan-pandavani:scenes:2:text","title":"The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata · 3 of 8","body":"And the name the whole world knows Pandavani by is Teejan Bai. She was born near Durg, in a Pardhi family, and as a small girl she would listen to her grandfather, Brijlal, singing the Mahabharata verses of the Chhattisgarhi tradition. She swallowed them whole — hours of epic, learned by ear, the way her land had always passed it down.","art":"art/story/sm/it-teejan-pandavani.jpg","route":"#/story/it.teejan-pandavani|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.teejan-pandavani-3":{"src":"story:it.teejan-pandavani:scenes:3:text","title":"The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata · 4 of 8","body":"But there was a wall in her way. Women who sang Pandavani in those days sang in the vedmati style — seated, decorous, the epic delivered sitting down. The blazing, striding, mace-swinging performance style — kapalik — was for men. And seated was not how the epic burned inside Teejan Bai.","art":"art/story/sm/it-teejan-pandavani.jpg","route":"#/story/it.teejan-pandavani|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.teejan-pandavani-5":{"src":"story:it.teejan-pandavani:scenes:5:text","title":"The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata · 6 of 8","body":"From village stages she went on to the country's biggest, and then the world's — performing across India and far beyond, carrying Chhattisgarh's way of telling the Mahabharata to audiences who spoke not a word of Chhattisgarhi and sat spellbound anyway. The nation gave her its highest artistic honours, Padma awards among them.","art":"art/story/sm/it-teejan-pandavani.jpg","route":"#/story/it.teejan-pandavani|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.teejan-pandavani-6":{"src":"story:it.teejan-pandavani:scenes:6:text","title":"The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata · 7 of 8","body":"And behind her, the door she opened stayed open. Girls across Chhattisgarh now learn Pandavani in both styles, seated and striding, many of them naming Teejan Bai as the reason they dared. The tradition did not break when she stood up. It grew a new branch, and the branch is full of singers.","art":"art/story/sm/it-teejan-pandavani.jpg","route":"#/story/it.teejan-pandavani|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Sang the Mahabharata","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ghotul-lamps-1":{"src":"story:fk.ghotul-lamps:scenes:1:text","title":"The Children's Own House · 2 of 8","body":"In the evenings, when the day's work is done, the village's boys and girls gather there — and the grown-ups stay away. That is the standing rule, kept by the elders themselves. Inside the ghotul, the young run everything.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ghotul-lamps.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ghotul-lamps|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children's Own House","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ghotul-lamps-2":{"src":"story:fk.ghotul-lamps:scenes:2:text","title":"The Children's Own House · 3 of 8","body":"And run it they do, properly. The ghotul has its own chosen leaders from among the young people, its own duties shared out — sweeping, firewood, repairs — its own code of conduct, and its own consequences for slacking, decided by the members. A small working republic, with a thatched roof.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ghotul-lamps.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ghotul-lamps|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children's Own House","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ghotul-lamps-3":{"src":"story:fk.ghotul-lamps:scenes:3:text","title":"The Children's Own House · 4 of 8","body":"A visitor once asked a Muria elder why the village hands its young an entire house and steps back. The elder found the question as strange as the visitor found the ghotul.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ghotul-lamps.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ghotul-lamps|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children's Own House","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ghotul-lamps-5":{"src":"story:fk.ghotul-lamps:scenes:5:text","title":"The Children's Own House · 6 of 8","body":"Younger children look up to older ones and are taught by them; older ones learn the patience of teaching. Quarrels are settled inside, by ghotul rules, without an adult voice in the room. Anyone who has ever run a clubhouse, a team or a classroom monitor system has touched a corner of what the ghotul does whole.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ghotul-lamps.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ghotul-lamps|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children's Own House","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ghotul-lamps-6":{"src":"story:fk.ghotul-lamps:scenes:6:text","title":"The Children's Own House · 7 of 8","body":"The ghotul is old — how old, nobody can say — and it has changed as Bastar changes; in some villages it thrives, in others it has faded, and the Muria themselves decide its future, as they always have. Scholars who came to study it went home describing one of the most remarkable schools of community life anywhere on Earth.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ghotul-lamps.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ghotul-lamps|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children's Own House","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bastar-chariot-1":{"src":"story:fk.bastar-chariot:scenes:1:text","title":"The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls · 2 of 8","body":"And here is the first surprise for visitors: Bastar Dussehra is not about a victory in the Ramayana. It belongs to the Devi — above all to Danteshwari, the great mother goddess of Bastar, whom the whole land honours as its own. The festival is Bastar gathering to its goddess, in Bastar's own way.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bastar-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bastar-chariot|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bastar-chariot-2":{"src":"story:fk.bastar-chariot:scenes:2:text","title":"The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls · 3 of 8","body":"At its heart rolls the rath: a massive chariot of solid wood on great solid wheels, tall as a house — built completely new each year. Trees are brought from the forest with due ceremony, and hereditary carpenter families from particular villages shape them with hand tools, as their fathers and grandfathers did, each village keeping the task that has been its honour for generations.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bastar-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bastar-chariot|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bastar-chariot-3":{"src":"story:fk.bastar-chariot:scenes:3:text","title":"The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls · 4 of 8","body":"Then comes the pulling. No engine, no animals. Long, heavy ropes — and hundreds of young men of the Adivasi communities, Muria and others, who come in from the villages holding the hereditary right to pull the rath, take their places on the lines.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bastar-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bastar-chariot|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bastar-chariot-5":{"src":"story:fk.bastar-chariot:scenes:5:text","title":"The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls · 6 of 8","body":"There is even a famous night, beloved of every child who hears the schedule, when the rath is spirited away and ceremonially \"stolen\" — and must be found and brought back with negotiation and feasting. Bastar has been running this festival so long it has built comedy into the liturgy.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bastar-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bastar-chariot|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bastar-chariot-6":{"src":"story:fk.bastar-chariot:scenes:6:text","title":"The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls · 7 of 8","body":"When it all ends, the Devi is seen off with honour until the next year, the rath's duty is done, and the carpenter villages already know that next year the forest will give new trees and their hands will build it all again. Nothing is stored. Everything is renewed. That is the Bastar way.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bastar-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bastar-chariot|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot the Whole Forest Pulls","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.myna-judge-1":{"src":"story:fk.myna-judge:scenes:1:text","title":"The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel · 2 of 8","body":"On the edge of a forest village stood a mango tree, and on either side of the mango tree lived two neighbours, and between them stood a quarrel so old it had practically built its own house. Whose tree? Whose mangoes? Every season the shouting came back with the fruit.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-myna-judge.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.myna-judge|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.myna-judge-2":{"src":"story:fk.myna-judge:scenes:2:text","title":"The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel · 3 of 8","body":"They shouted so long and loud that year that the whole forest knew the speeches by heart. And in the mango tree itself, listening with professional interest, sat a hill myna.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-myna-judge.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.myna-judge|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.myna-judge-3":{"src":"story:fk.myna-judge:scenes:3:text","title":"The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel · 4 of 8","body":"One morning the neighbours arrived to find the myna on the lowest branch, waiting like a magistrate. \"The forest has appointed me judge,\" she announced — in a voice of great authority, which she had borrowed from the village headwoman. \"Present your cases. I will consider every word.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-myna-judge.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.myna-judge|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.myna-judge-5":{"src":"story:fk.myna-judge:scenes:5:text","title":"The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel · 6 of 8","body":"The second neighbour fared no better. Her own speech, returned with perfect fidelity, contained — she now noticed — three insults, one exaggeration the size of the tree itself, and nothing whatsoever about mangoes. The myna waited, bright-eyed, entirely silent except for other people's words.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-myna-judge.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.myna-judge|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.myna-judge-6":{"src":"story:fk.myna-judge:scenes:6:text","title":"The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel · 7 of 8","body":"What could they do? Hearing yourself is a strong medicine. The two of them looked at each other over the roots, embarrassed into honesty at last, and settled the matter in ten minutes flat: the tree was nobody's and the fruit was everybody's, shared down the middle, with a basket for the judge.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-myna-judge.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.myna-judge|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Myna Who Judged the Quarrel","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.agaria-first-fire-1":{"src":"story:fk.agaria-first-fire:scenes:1:text","title":"The People of the First Furnace · 2 of 8","body":"Persuade is the right word, in the Agaria understanding. Iron is not a dead thing to them. In the furnace, their tradition holds, dwells Lohasur — the iron spirit, the lord of the metal — and no smelting begins without honouring him first. You do not barge into a forge. You greet the one who lives there.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agaria-first-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agaria-first-fire|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The People of the First Furnace","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.agaria-first-fire-2":{"src":"story:fk.agaria-first-fire:scenes:2:text","title":"The People of the First Furnace · 3 of 8","body":"Watch the old craft, where it survives: a small clay furnace built like a standing person, packed with layers of ore and charcoal; the goat-skin bellows worked in rhythm for hours, breathing for the fire; and then the moment — the furnace opened, and out of what was stone comes a glowing sponge of real iron, hissing like something being born.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agaria-first-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agaria-first-fire|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The People of the First Furnace","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.agaria-first-fire-3":{"src":"story:fk.agaria-first-fire:scenes:3:text","title":"The People of the First Furnace · 4 of 8","body":"An Agaria elder, teaching a boy the bellows, was asked by him why they thank the fire and the iron before beginning, every single time, when the family has done this ten thousand times.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agaria-first-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agaria-first-fire|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The People of the First Furnace","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.agaria-first-fire-5":{"src":"story:fk.agaria-first-fire:scenes:5:text","title":"The People of the First Furnace · 6 of 8","body":"The Agaria's own tellings of the first furnace and the first iron are theirs, and they are rich — full of the iron spirit, the first smiths, and hard bargains between fire and people. Scholars wrote some of them down generations ago; the community's own elders remain the true keepers, and versions differ village to village, as living lore does.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agaria-first-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agaria-first-fire|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The People of the First Furnace","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.agaria-first-fire-6":{"src":"story:fk.agaria-first-fire:scenes:6:text","title":"The People of the First Furnace · 7 of 8","body":"Big mills make India's iron now, and the old clay furnaces are few. But the knowledge that a person can build a furnace from riverbank clay and breathe stone into metal — that lives where it always lived: in Agaria memory, and in the hands of those who still demonstrate the old smelting for the young to see.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agaria-first-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agaria-first-fire|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The People of the First Furnace","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chitrakote-sings-1":{"src":"story:fk.chitrakote-sings:scenes:1:text","title":"The Waterfall That Sings · 2 of 8","body":"The villages of the country around have lived with the falls forever, and they will tell you what every visitor eventually notices: Chitrakote never sounds the same twice. It has seasons of voice, as a person does. This is the telling one riverside grandfather gave his granddaughter about that.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chitrakote-sings.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chitrakote-sings|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Waterfall That Sings","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chitrakote-sings-2":{"src":"story:fk.chitrakote-sings:scenes:2:text","title":"The Waterfall That Sings · 3 of 8","body":"\"In the monsoon,\" he said, \"she is a crowd — the whole brown flood shouting over the edge at once, and you must shout too, to be heard beside her. In autumn she is a choir, white and many-voiced. And in the dry months she narrows to silver threads, and plays soft, like a flute at the end of a feast.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chitrakote-sings.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chitrakote-sings|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Waterfall That Sings","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chitrakote-sings-3":{"src":"story:fk.chitrakote-sings:scenes:3:text","title":"The Waterfall That Sings · 4 of 8","body":"The girl asked the good question: \"Why so many songs? The rocks are the same rocks all year.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chitrakote-sings.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chitrakote-sings|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Waterfall That Sings","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chitrakote-sings-5":{"src":"story:fk.chitrakote-sings:scenes:5:text","title":"The Waterfall That Sings · 6 of 8","body":"The Indravati is Bastar's great river — the giver of fields and fish for the communities along her whole length — and the falls are her grandest public appearance. On full-moon nights the mist over the drop goes silver, and the pools below hold the moon like a coin, and nobody hurries home.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chitrakote-sings.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chitrakote-sings|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Waterfall That Sings","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chitrakote-sings-6":{"src":"story:fk.chitrakote-sings:scenes:6:text","title":"The Waterfall That Sings · 7 of 8","body":"Travellers come now from across the world with cameras, and the villages sell them tea and watch them discover, one by one, what Bastar always knew: you do not really photograph Chitrakote. You stand in the spray and let her sing whichever season's song she is practising, and you take that home instead.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chitrakote-sings.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chitrakote-sings|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Waterfall That Sings","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.danteshwari-bastar-1":{"src":"story:fk.danteshwari-bastar:scenes:1:text","title":"The Goddess Who Walked Behind · 2 of 8","body":"Long ago, the telling goes, a king called Annamdeva was leaving his old kingdom to found a new one in the southern forests. Before he set out, he prayed to the goddess his family had always served — and she gave him a promise, and a condition, which is how promises from goddesses usually arrive.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-danteshwari-bastar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.danteshwari-bastar|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Goddess Who Walked Behind","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.danteshwari-bastar-2":{"src":"story:fk.danteshwari-bastar:scenes:2:text","title":"The Goddess Who Walked Behind · 3 of 8","body":"\"I will come with you,\" she said. \"I will walk behind you, and where my feet go, your kingdom will be. You will not see me — but you will hear my anklets at your back, every step. Walk ahead, and trust the sound. And do not look back at me. That is the whole of the condition.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-danteshwari-bastar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.danteshwari-bastar|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Goddess Who Walked Behind","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.danteshwari-bastar-3":{"src":"story:fk.danteshwari-bastar:scenes:3:text","title":"The Goddess Who Walked Behind · 4 of 8","body":"So the king walked south, day upon day, through forests and over hills — and always behind him, chham, chham, the silver sound of the anklets, steady as his own heartbeat. Until he came to a place where two rivers meet, and crossed into the shallows, and there — the sound stopped.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-danteshwari-bastar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.danteshwari-bastar|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Goddess Who Walked Behind","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.danteshwari-bastar-5":{"src":"story:fk.danteshwari-bastar:scenes:5:text","title":"The Goddess Who Walked Behind · 6 of 8","body":"And so the temple of Danteshwari stands to this day at Dantewada, at the meeting of the rivers called Shankhini and Dankini, on the very spot where the anklets fell silent — and the town itself bears her name. The king built his kingdom around her, which is what the promise had meant all along.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-danteshwari-bastar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.danteshwari-bastar|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Goddess Who Walked Behind","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.danteshwari-bastar-6":{"src":"story:fk.danteshwari-bastar:scenes:6:text","title":"The Goddess Who Walked Behind · 7 of 8","body":"Each year at Bastar Dussehra, the great seventy-five-day festival, Danteshwari is brought with honour to Jagdalpur, and all Bastar gathers to her. The whole festival, grandest in the land, turns around the goddess who once walked behind a king with silver at her ankles.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-danteshwari-bastar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.danteshwari-bastar|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Goddess Who Walked Behind","facts":[["From","The Heart of the Land"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tejimola-1":{"src":"story:fk.tejimola:scenes:1:text","title":"Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming · 2 of 10","body":"Her father married again, and then the season came for him to take his boat down the Brahmaputra to trade. \"Look after my Tejimola,\" he said. The new wife smiled with her mouth. The boat went, and the house went quiet.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tejimola.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tejimola|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.tejimola-2":{"src":"story:fk.tejimola:scenes:2:text","title":"Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming · 3 of 10","body":"The stepmother's kindness left with the boat. Hard words, hard chores, and one day she sent Tejimola to pound paddy at the dheki — the great wooden rice-pounder that rises and falls like a see-saw. There the old telling turns harsh, and this one will not linger. The stepmother tried to make Tejimola gone. That is all that needs saying — because of what happened next.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tejimola.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tejimola|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.tejimola-3":{"src":"story:fk.tejimola:scenes:3:text","title":"Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming · 4 of 10","body":"By the fence where it happened, a gourd vine came up overnight — glossy, quick, stronger than any vine has a right to be. A neighbour woman stopped to pick a gourd, and the whole vine trembled and sang: \"Do not pick me, aunty. I am no gourd. I am Tejimola.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tejimola.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tejimola|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.tejimola-4":{"src":"story:fk.tejimola:scenes:4:text","title":"Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming · 5 of 10","body":"The stepmother heard of it, tore the vine up and flung it behind the house. Within days a bogori stood there — a wild plum tree — hung with fruit. Children reached for the plums and the branches swayed and sang: \"Do not pick me. I am no plum tree. I am Tejimola.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tejimola.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tejimola|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.tejimola-6":{"src":"story:fk.tejimola:scenes:6:text","title":"Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming · 7 of 10","body":"Months later a trading boat came up the river, and the merchant saw the flower and wanted it. But when the boatman reached out, the lotus drifted away. When the merchant himself leaned from the boat, it drifted close. And he heard — so small he felt it more than heard it — a voice he knew.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tejimola.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tejimola|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.tejimola-7":{"src":"story:fk.tejimola:scenes:7:text","title":"Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming · 8 of 10","body":"The lotus folded, and a mynah flew up out of the water and landed on his palm, and would not leave his shoulder the whole way home. At his own door he said, \"Now all of it. Your own shape.\" And there stood Tejimola — thinner, older about the eyes, and singing before she was done becoming.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tejimola.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tejimola|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.tejimola-8":{"src":"story:fk.tejimola:scenes:8:text","title":"Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming · 9 of 10","body":"She told him everything, plainly, the way she did all things. The stepmother was sent back to her own village, and that was the whole of it — the house did not need shouting, it needed its singing back, and it got it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tejimola.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tejimola|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tejimola, Who Kept Becoming","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.bordoisila-1":{"src":"story:fk.bordoisila:scenes:1:text","title":"The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother · 2 of 8","body":"Bordoisila, the telling goes, is a daughter — married far away, the way daughters used to be, a whole year of hills and rivers from her mother's house. All year she works in her husband's village. All year she waits.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bordoisila.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bordoisila|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bordoisila-2":{"src":"story:fk.bordoisila:scenes:2:text","title":"The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother · 3 of 8","body":"And when Bohag comes — the first month of the Assamese year, the month of Bihu — she is allowed to go home to her mother. She does not walk. She runs. Over the rivers, through the bamboo, skirts flying, hair loose, knocking things over the whole way and not stopping to pick up a single one of them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bordoisila.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bordoisila|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bordoisila-3":{"src":"story:fk.bordoisila:scenes:3:text","title":"The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother · 4 of 8","body":"\"That is why the roofs get tied down before Bihu,\" the grandmother says. \"Not because the wind is angry. Because a girl who has not seen her mother in a year does not slow down for your fence.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bordoisila.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bordoisila|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bordoisila-5":{"src":"story:fk.bordoisila:scenes:5:text","title":"The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother · 6 of 8","body":"But a visit is a visit. After the festival she has to go back — and there is a second wind then, a slower one, that drags its feet and drips with rain.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bordoisila.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bordoisila|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bordoisila-6":{"src":"story:fk.bordoisila:scenes:6:text","title":"The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother · 7 of 8","body":"So nobody in Assam curses the spring storm, or not for long. They tie the roof, they bring in the chillies drying on the mat, and they say: let her come. It has been a year.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bordoisila.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bordoisila|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wind Who Runs Home to Her Mother","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.xorai-elephant-1":{"src":"story:fk.xorai-elephant:scenes:1:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant · 2 of 9","body":"Through that forest walked an elephant who had never once been told no. \"I go where I like,\" he said, mostly to himself, since nobody walked with him. And he liked to scratch his back on the simolu tree — hard — without looking up.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-xorai-elephant.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.xorai-elephant|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.xorai-elephant-2":{"src":"story:fk.xorai-elephant:scenes:2:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant · 3 of 9","body":"The tree shook, the branch swung, and the nest came down, eggs and all. The elephant did not notice. That was the worst part, the sparrow said afterwards — not that he was big, but that he did not even notice.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-xorai-elephant.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.xorai-elephant|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.xorai-elephant-3":{"src":"story:fk.xorai-elephant:scenes:3:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant · 4 of 9","body":"She sat on the empty branch and cried, and her crying brought her friends: the woodpecker, the little bee, and the frog from the black pool. \"Crying is right,\" said the frog. \"And crying has friends. Now listen.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-xorai-elephant.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.xorai-elephant|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.xorai-elephant-5":{"src":"story:fk.xorai-elephant:scenes:5:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant · 6 of 9","body":"He walked toward the croaking without looking — of course without looking — and the bog took him to the shoulders and held him like a fist. He pulled all night and got nowhere. It was the first night of his life at somebody else's mercy, and it was very long.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-xorai-elephant.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.xorai-elephant|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.xorai-elephant-6":{"src":"story:fk.xorai-elephant:scenes:6:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant · 7 of 9","body":"At dawn the sparrow flew down and sat by his ear, small as a leaf. \"Do you know me?\" He thought about it. And then, cold and tired and shoulder-deep in mud, he did — the tree, the branch, the nest he had not noticed. \"I am sorry,\" he said, and meant it, which was another first.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-xorai-elephant.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.xorai-elephant|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.xorai-elephant-7":{"src":"story:fk.xorai-elephant:scenes:7:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant · 8 of 9","body":"It took the buffaloes treading the bank down, and branches under his feet, and most of a morning. He came out grey with mud and completely changed. Ever after, that elephant walked slowly, and looked up before he touched a tree — and it was that same elephant, people say, who would lift a fallen nest back onto its branch with the tip of his trunk.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-xorai-elephant.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.xorai-elephant|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Sparrow, the Frog, and the Very Big Elephant","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.burhi-aair-sadhu-1":{"src":"story:fk.burhi-aair-sadhu:scenes:1:text","title":"The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down · 2 of 8","body":"Then came printing presses, and schoolbooks, and the schoolbooks were in other people's languages. And a strange idea crept in with them: that stories in books were the real ones, and grandmother stories were small — nice, but small. Nobody said it out loud. It just settled, like dust.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-burhi-aair-sadhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.burhi-aair-sadhu|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.burhi-aair-sadhu-2":{"src":"story:fk.burhi-aair-sadhu:scenes:2:text","title":"The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down · 3 of 8","body":"A boy who had grown up inside those evenings became a writer. His name was Lakshminath Bezbaroa, and he wrote many things — but he kept coming back to one thought. Every time an old woman in Assam died, a shelf of stories burned down with her, and no one was even counting the fires.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-burhi-aair-sadhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.burhi-aair-sadhu|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.burhi-aair-sadhu-3":{"src":"story:fk.burhi-aair-sadhu:scenes:3:text","title":"The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down · 4 of 8","body":"So he decided to gather the tales the grandmothers told and put them in a book. And straight away there was a choice to make, and everything hung on it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-burhi-aair-sadhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.burhi-aair-sadhu|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.burhi-aair-sadhu-5":{"src":"story:fk.burhi-aair-sadhu:scenes:5:text","title":"The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down · 6 of 8","body":"And it worked. A hundred years and more later, every Assamese child still meets Tejimola — at home if they are lucky, in that book if the fireside is far away. The evenings got a second place to live.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-burhi-aair-sadhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.burhi-aair-sadhu|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.burhi-aair-sadhu-6":{"src":"story:fk.burhi-aair-sadhu:scenes:6:text","title":"The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down · 7 of 8","body":"Assam honours him with a title: Sahityarathi, the charioteer of literature. But ask what he actually did, and the honest answer is smaller and better. He listened to grandmothers, and he thought what they said was worth exactly as much care as anything ever printed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-burhi-aair-sadhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.burhi-aair-sadhu|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother's Stories, Written Down","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bihu-goru-1":{"src":"story:fk.bihu-goru:scenes:1:text","title":"The Morning the Cows Go First · 2 of 8","body":"It is Goru Bihu — the cattle's Bihu. Before anyone puts on anything new, the cows and bullocks are taken down to the river or the pond, every one of them, and given the best bath of their year.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bihu-goru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bihu-goru|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Morning the Cows Go First","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bihu-goru-2":{"src":"story:fk.bihu-goru:scenes:2:text","title":"The Morning the Cows Go First · 3 of 8","body":"A grandfather — a koka — stands knee-deep with a small boy and a large cow. \"Rub here. Behind the ears. She has carried this family all year, she can have your two arms for one morning.\" The cow, for her part, looks extremely dignified about the whole thing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bihu-goru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bihu-goru|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Morning the Cows Go First","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bihu-goru-3":{"src":"story:fk.bihu-goru:scenes:3:text","title":"The Morning the Cows Go First · 4 of 8","body":"They are washed, and rubbed with a paste of turmeric and black gram, and touched with sprigs of the proper leaves — every family knows which — and then comes the song, half-sung and half-said, that children have shouted at cows on this morning for generations: \"Lau kha, bengena kha, bosore bosore barhi ja.\" Eat gourd, eat brinjal, grow year upon year.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bihu-goru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bihu-goru|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Morning the Cows Go First","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bihu-goru-5":{"src":"story:fk.bihu-goru:scenes:5:text","title":"The Morning the Cows Go First · 6 of 8","body":"The next day is Manuh Bihu — the people's day. New clothes, and the giving of gamosas: the red-and-white woven cloth of Assam, laid over a person's hands or shoulders, which is how respect looks when you can hold it. The young bow to the elders and collect their blessings for the year.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bihu-goru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bihu-goru|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Morning the Cows Go First","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bihu-goru-6":{"src":"story:fk.bihu-goru:scenes:6:text","title":"The Morning the Cows Go First · 7 of 8","body":"And then the singing starts and does not really stop — husori parties going house to house with dhol and pepa, the drum and the buffalo-horn pipe, blessing each courtyard they land in. The dancing in the open — the bihu naach — is the thing people cross oceans to be home for.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bihu-goru.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bihu-goru|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Morning the Cows Go First","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ali-aye-ligang-1":{"src":"story:fk.ali-aye-ligang:scenes:1:text","title":"The Day the Sowing Begins · 2 of 8","body":"And when spring comes, the Mising keep Ali-Aye-Ligang. The name is the whole festival in three words of the Mising tongue: ali, the roots and seed; aye, the grain; ligang, the beginning of the sowing. The day the year's work starts.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ali-aye-ligang.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ali-aye-ligang|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day the Sowing Begins","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ali-aye-ligang-2":{"src":"story:fk.ali-aye-ligang:scenes:2:text","title":"The Day the Sowing Begins · 3 of 8","body":"On the morning itself, an elder of the family goes to the field and sows the first pinch of ahu rice — just a pinch, put into the earth with a word of thanks before anybody sows in earnest. The first handful of the season is a gift before it is a crop; the hills taught everyone that, and the river people did not forget it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ali-aye-ligang.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ali-aye-ligang|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day the Sowing Begins","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ali-aye-ligang-3":{"src":"story:fk.ali-aye-ligang:scenes:3:text","title":"The Day the Sowing Begins · 4 of 8","body":"Then the drums come out, and the gumrag is danced — lines of dancers, the beat going down through their heels into the ground, round and round in the open air. Rice is cooked wrapped in leaves, there is food in every house, and the whole village is in and out of everybody's door.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ali-aye-ligang.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ali-aye-ligang|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day the Sowing Begins","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ali-aye-ligang-5":{"src":"story:fk.ali-aye-ligang:scenes:5:text","title":"The Day the Sowing Begins · 6 of 8","body":"The seed goes in. The rains will come — on the Brahmaputra they always come, sometimes far too well — and the stilt houses will stand over their own reflections, and by autumn the pinch of rice given away in spring will have become the harvest.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ali-aye-ligang.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ali-aye-ligang|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day the Sowing Begins","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ali-aye-ligang-6":{"src":"story:fk.ali-aye-ligang:scenes:6:text","title":"The Day the Sowing Begins · 7 of 8","body":"And when the harvest does come in, the year closes the circle with a second festival of its own — because a people who dance the sowing are certainly not going to let the reaping pass quietly. Beginnings and endings both get drums; it is only the middle that has to make do with work.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ali-aye-ligang.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ali-aye-ligang|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day the Sowing Begins","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bwisagu-1":{"src":"story:fk.bwisagu:scenes:1:text","title":"Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance · 2 of 8","body":"And Bwisagu also begins with the cattle — washed, fussed over, thanked, set loose to wander like retired elders for a day. Across the whole valley, whatever the language of the house, the new year starts by being kind to the animals who work. That is worth noticing. Neighbours do not need to be the same to be wise in the same direction.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bwisagu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bwisagu|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bwisagu-2":{"src":"story:fk.bwisagu:scenes:2:text","title":"Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance · 3 of 8","body":"Then the people's days: new clothes, the young touching the feet of the old, visits and food and forgiveness — old quarrels are supposed to be left in the old year, which is easier said than done, which is why there is a festival to help.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bwisagu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bwisagu|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bwisagu-3":{"src":"story:fk.bwisagu:scenes:3:text","title":"Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance · 4 of 8","body":"And then the music, which nobody who has heard it forgets: the kham, the long drum; the sifung, the bamboo flute, low and breathy like wind deciding to sing; and the serja, the little bowed fiddle. Girls in the dokhona — the Bodo woven dress, gold and orange like the season itself — form up in lines.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bwisagu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bwisagu|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bwisagu-5":{"src":"story:fk.bwisagu:scenes:5:text","title":"Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance · 6 of 8","body":"In the courtyards of families who keep Bathou — the Bodo's own old faith — stands the sijou tree, planted and tended by the house, and the new year greets it too. Other Bodo families keep other faiths now, and the drums do not mind; Bwisagu is the year turning, and the year turns for everyone.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bwisagu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bwisagu|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bwisagu-6":{"src":"story:fk.bwisagu:scenes:6:text","title":"Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance · 7 of 8","body":"By evening the lines of dancers have joined into rings, and the sifung is handed from player to player so the tune never has to stop for anything as small as a person getting tired. A new year should be welcomed until the welcomers wobble; on this the Bodo villages and every other village on earth agree.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bwisagu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bwisagu|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Bwisagu and the Butterfly Dance","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sohpetbneng-1":{"src":"story:fk.sohpetbneng:scenes:1:text","title":"The Navel of the Sky · 2 of 8","body":"In the beginning, the telling goes, sixteen families lived above, in the sky country. And from the top of Lum Sohpetbneng rose a golden ladder — some tellings say a great tree — and the sixteen families went up and down it as freely as you go between the kitchen and the veranda.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sohpetbneng.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sohpetbneng|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Navel of the Sky","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sohpetbneng-2":{"src":"story:fk.sohpetbneng:scenes:2:text","title":"The Navel of the Sky · 3 of 8","body":"Down here was the garden: rivers to mind, forests to keep, broom-grass bending on the slopes, and the whole green work of the earth. The families would come down in the morning to tend it and climb home to the sky at night. Earth was the day's work. Heaven was supper.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sohpetbneng.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sohpetbneng|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Navel of the Sky","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sohpetbneng-3":{"src":"story:fk.sohpetbneng:scenes:3:text","title":"The Navel of the Sky · 4 of 8","body":"But a garden tended by visitors is never quite loved. And the day came when it had to be settled: who would truly take the earth — live on it, belong to it, keep it always?","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sohpetbneng.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sohpetbneng|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Navel of the Sky","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sohpetbneng-5":{"src":"story:fk.sohpetbneng:scenes:5:text","title":"The Navel of the Sky · 6 of 8","body":"And then the ladder was lost. The tellings differ about how, and elders will tell you in their own way and their own time. What every telling agrees on is what it meant: the easy road between earth and heaven closed, and the seven families kept the earth with their hands and the sky with their memory.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sohpetbneng.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sohpetbneng|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Navel of the Sky","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sohpetbneng-6":{"src":"story:fk.sohpetbneng:scenes:6:text","title":"The Navel of the Sky · 7 of 8","body":"They did not lose the hill, though. Every year, when winter loosens, people climb Lum Sohpetbneng together — up through the pines to the top, to stand where the ladder stood. Not to leave the earth. To remember that they came from higher up, and that the earth was not an exile but a choosing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sohpetbneng.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sohpetbneng|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Navel of the Sky","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.diengiei-1":{"src":"story:fk.diengiei:scenes:1:text","title":"The Tree That Shaded the Whole World · 2 of 9","body":"The rice would not ripen in the dark. The children went pale as mushrooms. It was not an angry tree — it was just a tree, being enormous — but the world underneath it was slowly going out.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-diengiei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.diengiei|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Shaded the Whole World","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.diengiei-2":{"src":"story:fk.diengiei:scenes:2:text","title":"The Tree That Shaded the Whole World · 3 of 9","body":"So the people took counsel, the way Khasis take counsel — everybody heard, nobody shouted — and it was agreed: Diengiei must come down. They took their axes to the west, and they cut from morning to dusk, and by nightfall the cut was deep and they went home sore and satisfied.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-diengiei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.diengiei|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Shaded the Whole World","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.diengiei-3":{"src":"story:fk.diengiei:scenes:3:text","title":"The Tree That Shaded the Whole World · 4 of 9","body":"At dawn they came back to smooth bark. No cut. Not a mark. They cut again all day — and again the morning healed it. Day after day. Axes do not lie, and trees do not mend themselves overnight.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-diengiei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.diengiei|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Shaded the Whole World","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.diengiei-5":{"src":"story:fk.diengiei:scenes:5:text","title":"The Tree That Shaded the Whole World · 6 of 9","body":"\"So do this,\" said the bird. \"Tonight, when you stop, do not carry your blades home. Leave them standing in the cut — edges outward.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-diengiei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.diengiei|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Shaded the Whole World","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.diengiei-6":{"src":"story:fk.diengiei:scenes:6:text","title":"The Tree That Shaded the Whole World · 7 of 9","body":"That night the tiger came padding through the dark to mend the wound as usual — and his tongue met cold, sharp edges instead of soft cut wood. He pulled back with his whiskers offended, decided this tree could look after itself, and took his beautiful angry tail elsewhere.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-diengiei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.diengiei|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Shaded the Whole World","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.diengiei-7":{"src":"story:fk.diengiei:scenes:7:text","title":"The Tree That Shaded the Whole World · 8 of 9","body":"The next day's cutting went deep into old work, and the day after that Diengiei came down like a hillside lying over. And the light came back — over the paddy, over the rivers, over everything, the whole held breath of the world let out at once.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-diengiei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.diengiei|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Shaded the Whole World","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.klew-peacock-1":{"src":"story:fk.klew-peacock:scenes:1:text","title":"Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes · 2 of 9","body":"The trouble with living in the sky is the view. Far below, U Klew could see the earth — the flooded paddy fields flashing like dropped mirrors, the ponds, the green. And a whisper reached him, the way whispers reach the restless, that life down there was finer than anything above.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-klew-peacock.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.klew-peacock|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.klew-peacock-2":{"src":"story:fk.klew-peacock:scenes:2:text","title":"Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes · 3 of 9","body":"\"One look,\" he told the sun's daughter. \"One look and I will come straight home.\" She did not argue. People who love you rarely argue with your one looks. She stood at the edge of the sky and watched him go down like a dropped jewel.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-klew-peacock.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.klew-peacock|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.klew-peacock-3":{"src":"story:fk.klew-peacock:scenes:3:text","title":"Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes · 4 of 9","body":"And the earth was lovely — that is the trap of it. The paddy was warm, the grain was easy, and in every still pond there was a handsome peacock looking up at him with obvious admiration. He stayed a day. Then a season. Then he stopped counting, because counting had begun to feel like an accusation.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-klew-peacock.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.klew-peacock|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.klew-peacock-5":{"src":"story:fk.klew-peacock:scenes:5:text","title":"Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes · 6 of 9","body":"So she did the thing this story is remembered for. She could not fetch him. She would not scold him. She had one thing that could still reach all the way down — her light.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-klew-peacock.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.klew-peacock|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.klew-peacock-6":{"src":"story:fk.klew-peacock:scenes:6:text","title":"Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes · 7 of 9","body":"Down through the morning it came, and where it fell on his trailing tail feathers it settled and stayed: a hundred small round marks of gold and green and blue. Eyes, people call them. A hundred little pictures of the sky's love, pinned to him where he could never lose them and never quite see them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-klew-peacock.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.klew-peacock|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.klew-peacock-7":{"src":"story:fk.klew-peacock:scenes:7:text","title":"Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes · 8 of 9","body":"And that, the Khasi elders say, is why the peacock does what he does at dawn. He turns to the east, where the sun comes up, and spreads the whole fan wide and holds it trembling — showing her, every single morning, that he still wears them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-klew-peacock.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.klew-peacock|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Peacock's Tail Is Full of Eyes","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ksew-market-1":{"src":"story:fk.ksew-market:scenes:1:text","title":"Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody · 2 of 8","body":"U Ksew the dog came too, with the one thing he had made himself: a jar of fermented pickle, dark and strong — the sort of smell you either love with your whole heart or run from with your whole legs. He set it down proudly at the end of the row.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ksew-market.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ksew-market|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ksew-market-2":{"src":"story:fk.ksew-market:scenes:2:text","title":"Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody · 3 of 8","body":"The big animals came down the row and reached his stall and made faces. \"What is THAT?\" Laughter is fine when it is with you. This was the other kind. And in the jostling and the mocking, somebody kicked the jar over, and it broke on the stones, and the whole market laughed harder.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ksew-market.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ksew-market|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ksew-market-3":{"src":"story:fk.ksew-market:scenes:3:text","title":"Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody · 4 of 8","body":"The dog went to the elders of the market for justice. They shrugged. \"It is only the dog's jar.\" Only. That word did more damage than the kick had.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ksew-market.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ksew-market|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ksew-market-5":{"src":"story:fk.ksew-market:scenes:5:text","title":"Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody · 6 of 8","body":"And that was that, for all time. The dog stayed. He got the fireside, the leftovers, the children to guard, a name called warmly across a field. The animals kept their market, and lost the most loyal heart that had ever stood in it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ksew-market.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ksew-market|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ksew-market-6":{"src":"story:fk.ksew-market:scenes:6:text","title":"Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody · 7 of 8","body":"But here is the ending Khasi children wait for. When the jar broke, the pickle splashed — onto the feet of every animal who had crowded in to laugh. And smells do not lie and do not fade, not to a nose like his.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ksew-market.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ksew-market|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Dog Sniffs Everybody","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.root-bridges-1":{"src":"story:fk.root-bridges:scenes:1:text","title":"The Bridge That Is Alive · 2 of 8","body":"Which is a problem, because the villages of the War Khasi and War Jaintia people sit deep in those gorges, and children cross rivers to school and farmers cross rivers to market. Bamboo washes away. Timber rots in a season. Iron rusts and eats itself. Everything you can build a bridge from, this weather destroys.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-root-bridges.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.root-bridges|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bridge That Is Alive","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.root-bridges-2":{"src":"story:fk.root-bridges:scenes:2:text","title":"The Bridge That Is Alive · 3 of 8","body":"Everything you can build from. So they grow them instead. A rubber-fig tree is planted by the bank — a tree that throws down long, patient roots from its branches. And the village begins to train those roots: coaxed through hollowed-out betel palm trunks, guided across the river a little further each year, tied, woven, wound back into each other.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-root-bridges.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.root-bridges|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bridge That Is Alive","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.root-bridges-3":{"src":"story:fk.root-bridges:scenes:3:text","title":"The Bridge That Is Alive · 4 of 8","body":"They call it a jingkieng jri — a living root bridge. And here is the price of it: fifteen years, twenty, sometimes more, before the roots are thick enough to carry a person. The one who plants the tree may never cross on it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-root-bridges.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.root-bridges|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bridge That Is Alive","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.root-bridges-5":{"src":"story:fk.root-bridges:scenes:5:text","title":"The Bridge That Is Alive · 6 of 8","body":"And once it carries, it never stops. A root bridge gets stronger every year — every flood it survives, it grips harder; every season, it thickens. There are bridges down in those gorges that have carried people for generations beyond counting, still growing. At Nongriat there is a famous one with two decks, one above the other, like a house that decided to be a bridge.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-root-bridges.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.root-bridges|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bridge That Is Alive","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.root-bridges-6":{"src":"story:fk.root-bridges:scenes:6:text","title":"The Bridge That Is Alive · 7 of 8","body":"No engineer signs a root bridge. A village does, over lifetimes — each generation tying in new roots, trimming, mending, handing it on. It is the patientest architecture on earth, and it is being practised right now, in the rain, by people who know exactly how long good things take.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-root-bridges.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.root-bridges|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bridge That Is Alive","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.wangala-1":{"src":"story:fk.wangala:scenes:1:text","title":"A Hundred Drums Say Thank You · 2 of 8","body":"All year the hill fields are worked, and by late autumn the harvest is finally in — grain in the baskets, the lean months beaten one more time. And now comes a rule as old as the fields: nobody eats freely of the new crop until thanks have been given to the one who gave it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-wangala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.wangala|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Hundred Drums Say Thank You","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.wangala-2":{"src":"story:fk.wangala:scenes:2:text","title":"A Hundred Drums Say Thank You · 3 of 8","body":"The thanks go to Misi Saljong, the great giver, who lends the seasons and the seed. The headman — the nokma — sees the first offerings made in his house: first fruits, first grain, given back before anything is taken. The order matters. Gift, then feast. Never the other way.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-wangala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.wangala|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Hundred Drums Say Thank You","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.wangala-3":{"src":"story:fk.wangala:scenes:3:text","title":"A Hundred Drums Say Thank You · 4 of 8","body":"And then Wangala begins, and Wangala is not quiet. They call it the festival of a hundred drums, and they are not exaggerating — the dama, the long oval drums, come out by the dozen and the dozen dozen, and the men drum standing in lines, and the sound rolls off the hills like the hills answering back.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-wangala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.wangala|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Hundred Drums Say Thank You","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.wangala-5":{"src":"story:fk.wangala:scenes:5:text","title":"A Hundred Drums Say Thank You · 6 of 8","body":"A child at her first Wangala asked the question children always ask: the rice is ours — we cleared the field, we weeded it, we cut it. Why does the first of it go away as a gift?","art":"art/story/sm/fk-wangala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.wangala|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Hundred Drums Say Thank You","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.wangala-6":{"src":"story:fk.wangala:scenes:6:text","title":"A Hundred Drums Say Thank You · 7 of 8","body":"These days there is a great gathering in the Garo hills each November where lines of drummers from many villages meet — a hundred drums genuinely all in one place, one of the great sights and sounds of the Northeast. People travel a long way for it, and stand very close to the drums, and regret nothing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-wangala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.wangala|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Hundred Drums Say Thank You","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.behdienkhlam-1":{"src":"story:fk.behdienkhlam:scenes:1:text","title":"Chasing the Sickness Out of Town · 2 of 8","body":"The enemy is khlam — plague, sickness, the fevers that used to walk into hill towns in the wet season when the paths were mud and the clouds sat on the roofs for weeks. You cannot see sickness coming. In the old days you could only watch it arrive.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-behdienkhlam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.behdienkhlam|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chasing the Sickness Out of Town","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.behdienkhlam-2":{"src":"story:fk.behdienkhlam:scenes:2:text","title":"Chasing the Sickness Out of Town · 3 of 8","body":"The Pnar answer is Behdienkhlam, and the name is the plan: beh dien khlam — to chase away the plague, to beat it out of town. It is kept by the families of the Niamtre, the Pnar's own faith, in the weeks after the sowing, when the rice is in the ground and the rain is doing its work.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-behdienkhlam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.behdienkhlam|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chasing the Sickness Out of Town","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.behdienkhlam-3":{"src":"story:fk.behdienkhlam:scenes:3:text","title":"Chasing the Sickness Out of Town · 4 of 8","body":"Young men go from house to house, and with long bamboo poles they beat on the rooftops — a tremendous knocking racket, on purpose — telling the sickness at every single house: not here, out you go, this roof is watched. Nobody's house is skipped. Chasing is a thing you do for everybody or not at all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-behdienkhlam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.behdienkhlam|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chasing the Sickness Out of Town","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.behdienkhlam-5":{"src":"story:fk.behdienkhlam:scenes:5:text","title":"Chasing the Sickness Out of Town · 6 of 8","body":"And they are carried down to a muddy pool called Aitnar, and there — in the rain, in the crowd, in mud to the knee — the carriers dance them, wading and heaving, while the whole town roars from the banks. The quiet half of the festival happens indoors, where families make their offerings; the loud half is very loud indeed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-behdienkhlam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.behdienkhlam|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chasing the Sickness Out of Town","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.behdienkhlam-6":{"src":"story:fk.behdienkhlam:scenes:6:text","title":"Chasing the Sickness Out of Town · 7 of 8","body":"On the last day comes datlawakor: a wooden ball, two sides of the town, and a game with no referee that is half football and half glorious struggle. Whichever side wins, they say, the harvest will favour their end of the valley.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-behdienkhlam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.behdienkhlam|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chasing the Sickness Out of Town","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chemtatrawta-1":{"src":"story:fk.chemtatrawta:scenes:1:text","title":"It All Started With a Lobster · 2 of 10","body":"Chemtatrawta sat by the stream one morning doing exactly that, sharpening his chem on a wet stone, minding absolutely nobody's business. And a lobster in the shallows — who tells this part differently, but never mind — reached out and pinched him, hard, on the hand.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chemtatrawta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chemtatrawta|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from It All Started With a Lobster","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chemtatrawta-2":{"src":"story:fk.chemtatrawta:scenes:2:text","title":"It All Started With a Lobster · 3 of 10","body":"He leapt up with a shout and slashed out wildly, and down came a stand of bamboo. The bamboo fell on the back of a jungle fowl, who exploded off her scratching-ground in a fury of feathers — straight through an ants' nest, kicking it open like a burst sack.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chemtatrawta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chemtatrawta|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from It All Started With a Lobster","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chemtatrawta-3":{"src":"story:fk.chemtatrawta:scenes:3:text","title":"It All Started With a Lobster · 4 of 10","body":"The ants, who take these things personally, marched out and bit the first large thing they found, which was a sleeping wild boar. The boar came up like a landslide and charged through the nearest plantain tree — where a fruit bat was asleep, upside down, dreaming of figs.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chemtatrawta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chemtatrawta|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from It All Started With a Lobster","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chemtatrawta-4":{"src":"story:fk.chemtatrawta:scenes:4:text","title":"It All Started With a Lobster · 5 of 10","body":"The bat flew off blind with sleep and went straight into the ear of the elephant. The elephant — who is enormously brave about everything except surprises inside his ear — bolted through the edge of the village, flattened an old woman's fence, trampled her cotton flat and smashed the water-gourds drying on her rack.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chemtatrawta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chemtatrawta|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from It All Started With a Lobster","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chemtatrawta-6":{"src":"story:fk.chemtatrawta:scenes:6:text","title":"It All Started With a Lobster · 7 of 10","body":"The village chief did what Mizo villages have always done with a proper mess: he called everyone in and held an inquiry, in order, out loud.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chemtatrawta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chemtatrawta|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from It All Started With a Lobster","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chemtatrawta-7":{"src":"story:fk.chemtatrawta:scenes:7:text","title":"It All Started With a Lobster · 8 of 10","body":"\"Elephant, why did you smash the gourds?\" \"Because the bat flew into my ear.\" \"Bat, why did you fly into his ear?\" \"Because the boar knocked down my tree.\" \"Boar?\" \"Ants bit me.\" \"Ants?\" \"The fowl kicked our nest.\" \"Fowl?\" \"The bamboo fell on me.\" \"Bamboo—\" and here everyone turned to Chemtatrawta, who turned rather red.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chemtatrawta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chemtatrawta|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from It All Started With a Lobster","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chemtatrawta-8":{"src":"story:fk.chemtatrawta:scenes:8:text","title":"It All Started With a Lobster · 9 of 10","body":"\"The lobster pinched me,\" he said. And the lobster, brought up from the stream in a pot, waved his whiskers and said the only honest thing said all day: \"A great hand came into my stream. I pinched before I looked.\" There was a silence, and then the whole village laughed at once, because every single creature in the chain had done exactly the same thing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chemtatrawta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chemtatrawta|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from It All Started With a Lobster","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-door-1":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-door:scenes:1:text","title":"Chhura Minds the Door · 2 of 9","body":"One day Chhura's family was invited to a feast in the next village — a proper one, with a pig in it. Somebody had to stay back, because in the hills you did not leave a house standing open. \"Chhura,\" said his wife, choosing her words with the care his wife had learned to use, \"stay here. Mind the door. Do not leave the door, whatever happens.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-door.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-door|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chhura Minds the Door","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-door-2":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-door:scenes:2:text","title":"Chhura Minds the Door · 3 of 9","body":"Chhura sat by the door. He minded it. It was a good door and he minded it thoroughly. An hour passed. The door did nothing, which is the main activity of doors.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-door.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-door|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chhura Minds the Door","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-door-3":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-door:scenes:3:text","title":"Chhura Minds the Door · 4 of 9","body":"And then the wind changed, and down the valley, faint but unmistakable, came the smell of the feast. Pork and smoke and roasting things. Chhura stood up. He sat down. He looked at the door. He had been given one instruction, and he was a man who took instructions seriously — that was never his problem. His problem was what he took them to mean.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-door.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-door|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chhura Minds the Door","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-door-5":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-door:scenes:5:text","title":"Chhura Minds the Door · 6 of 9","body":"The feast fell quiet as he came in. His wife looked at him, and looked at the door, and said — with the calm of long experience — \"Chhura. Why is the door at the feast?\" And Chhura, honestly baffled that it needed saying, told her: \"You said not to leave it. I have not left it for one moment. Ask the door.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-door.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-door|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chhura Minds the Door","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-door-6":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-door:scenes:6:text","title":"Chhura Minds the Door · 7 of 9","body":"The family ran all the way home, where the house had stood open all day to every creature in the hills. The hens were in the rice. The goat was on the bed, wearing an expression. It took till nightfall to put the house right, and Chhura helped, carrying the door, which he refused to be parted from until it was back on its pegs.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-door.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-door|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chhura Minds the Door","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-door-7":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-door:scenes:7:text","title":"Chhura Minds the Door · 8 of 9","body":"And nobody could stay cross with him — that is the thing about Chhura, and the reason a whole people has kept him fed with stories for generations. His mistakes were never lazy and never mean. He simply took every word you said and walked off with it in a direction you had not imagined words could go.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-door.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-door|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Chhura Minds the Door","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-nahaia-1":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-nahaia:scenes:1:text","title":"The Field That Hummed · 2 of 9","body":"The two of them cleared new fields that year — a lo each, a hill field cut from the slope, which is how farming is done in these mountains. And when the clearing was finished, Nahaia walked his own land and stopped dead. In the great hollow stump in the middle of his field hung a wild bees' nest the size of a rice basket, roaring quietly to itself.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-nahaia.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-nahaia|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Field That Hummed","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-nahaia-2":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-nahaia:scenes:2:text","title":"The Field That Hummed · 3 of 9","body":"Nahaia was not fond of stings. So he went to his brother wearing his sincerest face. \"Chhura. It pains me, but I will swap fields with you. Mine is the better land — you can hear it. My field hums with life. I will miss that hum terribly.\" And Chhura, who believed people, swapped on the spot.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-nahaia.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-nahaia|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Field That Hummed","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-nahaia-3":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-nahaia:scenes:3:text","title":"The Field That Hummed · 4 of 9","body":"Chhura walked his new field and heard the hum and followed it to the stump, and looked up at several thousand bees — khuai, the wild honeybees of the hills — going about their business over his head.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-nahaia.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-nahaia|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Field That Hummed","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-nahaia-5":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-nahaia:scenes:5:text","title":"The Field That Hummed · 6 of 9","body":"Then, on a cold misty morning when the bees were slow and sleepy in their nest, he came quietly with a smudge of green-leaf smoke, thanked them out loud — which the bees are not recorded as acknowledging — and took a fat share of the comb, leaving them theirs. He carried home honey by the potful. The sweetest in the valley, people said, and people were right.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-nahaia.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-nahaia|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Field That Hummed","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-nahaia-6":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-nahaia:scenes:6:text","title":"The Field That Hummed · 7 of 9","body":"Nahaia arrived at a run, having heard. He stood looking at the pots, doing the arithmetic of his own cleverness, and finding it came out owing. \"That,\" he said at last, \"was my field.\" \"It was,\" agreed Chhura pleasantly. \"The hummingest field a brother ever gave me.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-nahaia.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-nahaia|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Field That Hummed","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chhura-nahaia-7":{"src":"story:fk.chhura-nahaia:scenes:7:text","title":"The Field That Hummed · 8 of 9","body":"And then — because Chhura's heart was the one thing about him nobody ever called foolish — he cut a piece of comb and handed it over. Brothers are brothers, and honey is for sharing, even with the ones who meant you to be stung.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chhura-nahaia.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chhura-nahaia|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Field That Hummed","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ngaitei-1":{"src":"story:fk.ngaitei:scenes:1:text","title":"Ngaitei and the River · 2 of 9","body":"One afternoon the water around Ngaitei's ankles held on. Not roughly — like a hand, not unkind, but not letting go. And she went down into the green, down to where the light comes from nowhere in particular, and the quiet had her father's voice in it. He had missed her. The river-country keeps what it loves, and it had decided to keep her too.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ngaitei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ngaitei|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Ngaitei and the River","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ngaitei-2":{"src":"story:fk.ngaitei:scenes:2:text","title":"Ngaitei and the River · 3 of 9","body":"Her grandmother came to the bank before dark, and did not scream and did not run for the men with poles. She stood at the very lip of the water and spoke into it, the way you speak to a relative you respect and disagree with. \"She is all I have. You have your country and your company. Send her up.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ngaitei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ngaitei|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Ngaitei and the River","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ngaitei-3":{"src":"story:fk.ngaitei:scenes:3:text","title":"Ngaitei and the River · 4 of 9","body":"And the river — this is a Mizo river, old-fashioned, correct about debts — sent her up, with one condition, spoken in the way deep water speaks: she keeps nothing of mine. Nothing that belongs to this side of the surface goes up the bank with her. The grandmother agreed, and carried Ngaitei home wrapped in her own cloth, and life went on.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ngaitei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ngaitei|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Ngaitei and the River","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ngaitei-5":{"src":"story:fk.ngaitei:scenes:5:text","title":"Ngaitei and the River · 6 of 9","body":"The grandmother stood in her doorway with the water at the step, and made herself think like a river. We agreed. I keep my word. So what, in this house, is not ours?","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ngaitei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ngaitei|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Ngaitei and the River","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ngaitei-6":{"src":"story:fk.ngaitei:scenes:6:text","title":"Ngaitei and the River · 7 of 9","body":"Ngaitei went to her sleeping mat and brought it out from under the pillow: a wooden comb, dark with age. Her father's comb, from before. She had carried it up the bank inside her wrap, because it was all of him she had, and she had told nobody, because that is what we do with the things we cannot give up.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ngaitei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ngaitei|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Ngaitei and the River","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ngaitei-7":{"src":"story:fk.ngaitei:scenes:7:text","title":"Ngaitei and the River · 8 of 9","body":"They carried it to the water's edge together, and her grandmother put it on the surface gently, with a kind word, the way you hand something precious across a fence. The comb turned once and went under. And the river stopped. Stood a moment, the way a person stands when a long errand is finally done — and went home, past the bathing place, past the stones, back into its bed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ngaitei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ngaitei|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Ngaitei and the River","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.mauruangi-1":{"src":"story:fk.mauruangi:scenes:1:text","title":"Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree · 2 of 10","body":"The stepsister ate from the pot. Mauruangi got the scrapings and the water the rice was washed in, and the heavy work, and the cold end of the fire. It is an old unfairness; many tales carry it because many childhoods did.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mauruangi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mauruangi|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.mauruangi-2":{"src":"story:fk.mauruangi:scenes:2:text","title":"Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree · 3 of 10","body":"But here is what the stepmother could not understand: Mauruangi did not fade. Her eyes were bright, her cheeks were round, she sang on the path to the water. A girl fed on scraps, glowing like the harvest moon. It was, the stepmother felt, somehow insolent.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mauruangi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mauruangi|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.mauruangi-3":{"src":"story:fk.mauruangi:scenes:3:text","title":"Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree · 4 of 10","body":"The secret lived in the river. Every day, when Mauruangi came down with her pot, a great catfish rose out of the deep water and fed her — good rice, from its own mouth, warm as if from a hearth. \"Open your mouth, little one,\" it would say. And its voice was a voice she knew, from before remembering. The river had her mother; now you know what the river had done with her.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mauruangi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mauruangi|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.mauruangi-4":{"src":"story:fk.mauruangi:scenes:4:text","title":"Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree · 5 of 10","body":"The stepmother sent her own daughter to spy, and the secret came home, and what was done about the fish the old tellings say plainly and this one will not. The fish was gone. That is all. Mauruangi sat on the bank where it used to rise, and the water was just water.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mauruangi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mauruangi|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.mauruangi-6":{"src":"story:fk.mauruangi:scenes:6:text","title":"Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree · 7 of 10","body":"Whoever rested under that tree came away fed — not in the stomach, exactly, but in the place the stomach reports to. Mauruangi did her crying there and her singing there, and the tree dropped red flowers on her the way a mother pats a head.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mauruangi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mauruangi|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.mauruangi-7":{"src":"story:fk.mauruangi:scenes:7:text","title":"Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree · 8 of 10","body":"One day a lal — a chief, for that is the Mizo word — came riding past and heard a girl under a flowering tree, singing thanks to it by name. He stopped. \"Who feeds you, child?\" he asked, because he could see the house she came from and had done his own arithmetic.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mauruangi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mauruangi|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.mauruangi-8":{"src":"story:fk.mauruangi:scenes:8:text","title":"Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree · 9 of 10","body":"The lal heard her out, and looked a long time at the tree, and asked her to come to his house — as its lady, not its servant; the tellings agree she went gladly. And by her new door, the first thing she planted was a phunchawng seed. It is probably flowering there still, out of season, whenever somebody needs it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mauruangi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mauruangi|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Mauruangi and the Silk-Cotton Tree","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sichangneii-1":{"src":"story:fk.sichangneii:scenes:1:text","title":"The Winged One · 2 of 8","body":"A young man of the hills was watching from the trees with his breath held. And when the sisters rose and dressed in their wings and went up like sparks going home to a fire — one pair of wings still lay on the grass, because he had crept out and taken them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sichangneii.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sichangneii|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Winged One","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sichangneii-2":{"src":"story:fk.sichangneii:scenes:2:text","title":"The Winged One · 3 of 8","body":"He hid the wings deep in the thatch of his roof. And Sichangneii, who could not fly, stayed. Here the telling must be fair to him, because it is: he was kind in every other thing. He worked for her, sang with her, built the house tall. She married him. There were children, and there was laughter, and most days the sky was just weather.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sichangneii.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sichangneii|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Winged One","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sichangneii-3":{"src":"story:fk.sichangneii:scenes:3:text","title":"The Winged One · 4 of 8","body":"But on clear evenings she would stand in the doorway and watch the hawks going up the last light, all the way up, until they were nothing. And her husband would find something to do with his hands, because he knew what he knew, and the thatch was directly over both of them the whole time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sichangneii.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sichangneii|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Winged One","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sichangneii-5":{"src":"story:fk.sichangneii:scenes:5:text","title":"The Winged One · 6 of 8","body":"She did not shout, and she did not weep, and the tellings do not say she even hesitated long. The sky was her mother's country, and she had been kept from it — and keeping is not the same as loving, however kindly it is done.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sichangneii.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sichangneii|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Winged One","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sichangneii-6":{"src":"story:fk.sichangneii:scenes:6:text","title":"The Winged One · 7 of 8","body":"She put the wings on. She circled the house three times — the tellings keep that detail, three slow circles, low over the thatch, touching nothing and leaving nothing out — and then she went up, the way her sisters had gone all those years before, and the blue took her in.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sichangneii.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sichangneii|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Winged One","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.liandova-1":{"src":"story:fk.liandova:scenes:1:text","title":"The Two Brothers and the Great Snake · 2 of 10","body":"But whatever came into Liandova's hands went first to his brother's mouth. Always. A roasted root, a scrap of dried fish, one wing of a small bird — Tuaisiala ate before Liandova did, every single time, and if there was one mouthful only, Liandova was not hungry, he said. Brothers who have nothing else have this.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-liandova.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.liandova|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Brothers and the Great Snake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.liandova-2":{"src":"story:fk.liandova:scenes:2:text","title":"The Two Brothers and the Great Snake · 3 of 10","body":"When the village held its feasts, the brothers got the leavings and the far edge of the firelight. Nobody was cruel enough to send them away and nobody was kind enough to call them close, which is its own particular kind of cold.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-liandova.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.liandova|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Brothers and the Great Snake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.liandova-3":{"src":"story:fk.liandova:scenes:3:text","title":"The Two Brothers and the Great Snake · 4 of 10","body":"Then came the day of the great snake. Out gleaning far from the village, the brothers found a python — old, slow, enormous, thick as a roof-beam and long past its dangerous years. Where grown hunters had found nothing all season, two hungry boys came home dragging a catch it took half the village to believe.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-liandova.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.liandova|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Brothers and the Great Snake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.liandova-4":{"src":"story:fk.liandova:scenes:4:text","title":"The Two Brothers and the Great Snake · 5 of 10","body":"And inside it — this is the part the tellings lean close to say — they found what the old python had swallowed in its hundred years of swallowing: dar, the brass gongs, whose voices are wealth in these hills, and beads of the old kind, the kind a chief's wife wears. The poorest boys in the village stood in front of a fortune.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-liandova.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.liandova|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Brothers and the Great Snake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.liandova-6":{"src":"story:fk.liandova:scenes:6:text","title":"The Two Brothers and the Great Snake · 7 of 10","body":"Liandova had gongs, beads, and a memory that worked perfectly. And now he had a decision, because wealth in the Mizo hills has one great use: the feast of honour, where a man feeds the whole village and is counted great for it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-liandova.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.liandova|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Brothers and the Great Snake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.liandova-7":{"src":"story:fk.liandova:scenes:7:text","title":"The Two Brothers and the Great Snake · 8 of 10","body":"The feast Liandova gave is the kind the hills remember — the gongs sounding, the rice steaming, every household fed, the ones who had mocked him eating with their eyes carefully on their food. And at the head of it all, seated first, served first, little Tuaisiala, who could not quite stop grinning.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-liandova.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.liandova|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Brothers and the Great Snake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.liandova-8":{"src":"story:fk.liandova:scenes:8:text","title":"The Two Brothers and the Great Snake · 9 of 10","body":"Nobody's name was thrown back at them. That was the last of Liandova's wealth and the best of it: he could have bought a hundred humiliations that day, and he bought a village instead.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-liandova.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.liandova|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Brothers and the Great Snake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.chapchar-kut-1":{"src":"story:fk.chapchar-kut:scenes:1:text","title":"The Festival of the Waiting · 2 of 8","body":"And into exactly that pause the Mizo people long ago put their greatest festival: Chapchar Kut, the kut — the festival — of the waiting time. The story usually told about how it began is that a hunting party came home empty-handed, and the chief, rather than let the village sit glum in its idle days, called for a feast — and the feast was so good they kept it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chapchar-kut.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chapchar-kut|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of the Waiting","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chapchar-kut-2":{"src":"story:fk.chapchar-kut:scenes:2:text","title":"The Festival of the Waiting · 3 of 8","body":"The heart of Chapchar Kut is the cheraw, and the cheraw is worth crossing the country for. Long bamboo poles are laid in pairs on the ground, and the clappers kneel at their ends and clap them — open, shut, open, shut — in a rhythm that quickens like rain arriving.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chapchar-kut.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chapchar-kut|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of the Waiting","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chapchar-kut-3":{"src":"story:fk.chapchar-kut:scenes:3:text","title":"The Festival of the Waiting · 4 of 8","body":"And between those clapping bamboos, dancers step. In and out of the closing gaps, on the beat, girls in the bright woven puan stepping neat as birds, while the poles snap shut a finger's width behind each ankle.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chapchar-kut.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chapchar-kut|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of the Waiting","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chapchar-kut-5":{"src":"story:fk.chapchar-kut:scenes:5:text","title":"The Festival of the Waiting · 6 of 8","body":"Chapchar Kut faded for some decades — the hills changed faiths and changed rulers, and for a while the old festival lay as quiet as the drying bamboo. Then, within living memory, Mizos brought it back, deliberately, because a people is allowed to keep its own springtime.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chapchar-kut.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chapchar-kut|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of the Waiting","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.chapchar-kut-6":{"src":"story:fk.chapchar-kut:scenes:6:text","title":"The Festival of the Waiting · 7 of 8","body":"So today Mizoram is largely Christian, and Chapchar Kut fills Aizawl every March all the same — church choirs one day, cheraw the next, Sunday best and ancient weave in the same photograph, nobody embarrassed and nobody asked to choose. It is not a rival to anyone's faith. It is the hills remembering their year.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-chapchar-kut.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.chapchar-kut|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of the Waiting","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.unakoti-1":{"src":"story:fk.unakoti:scenes:1:text","title":"One Less Than a Crore · 2 of 9","body":"The place is called Unakoti, and the name is a number: one less than a koti — a crore, ten million. Ninety-nine lakh, ninety-nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine. Somebody, the name says, fell exactly one short of a crore. Two stories are told about who, and you may have them both.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-unakoti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.unakoti|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from One Less Than a Crore","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.unakoti-2":{"src":"story:fk.unakoti:scenes:2:text","title":"One Less Than a Crore · 3 of 9","body":"The first: Shiva was travelling to Kashi, and with him went a crore of gods and goddesses — a whole sky on the move. Night caught them in these hills, and they made camp. \"We leave at first light,\" said Shiva. \"All of us. Together.\" And the crore of travellers agreed, and yawned, and slept.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-unakoti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.unakoti|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from One Less Than a Crore","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.unakoti-3":{"src":"story:fk.unakoti:scenes:3:text","title":"One Less Than a Crore · 4 of 9","body":"At first light, one traveller woke: Shiva. One, out of a crore. The rest slept on in the sweet hill air, the way you sleep at your cousins' house when nobody wakes you. And Shiva looked at them all for a long moment — and went on to Kashi alone.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-unakoti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.unakoti|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from One Less Than a Crore","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.unakoti-5":{"src":"story:fk.unakoti:scenes:5:text","title":"One Less Than a Crore · 6 of 9","body":"The second story belongs to a craftsman: Kallu, a local sculptor, who longed to travel with Shiva and Parvati to their mountain. Take me with you, he begged. And he was set a maker's test: carve a full crore of images in a single night. Finish before the dawn, and come.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-unakoti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.unakoti|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from One Less Than a Crore","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.unakoti-6":{"src":"story:fk.unakoti:scenes:6:text","title":"One Less Than a Crore · 7 of 9","body":"Kallu carved like a man on fire. Faces bloomed out of the rock all night — great ones, small ones, gods by the thousand, the hill filling under his hands. And the dawn came up over the trees, and the count stood at one less than a crore. One. Less.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-unakoti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.unakoti|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from One Less Than a Crore","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.unakoti-7":{"src":"story:fk.unakoti:scenes:7:text","title":"One Less Than a Crore · 8 of 9","body":"So he could not go. But look what he could not go from: a hillside he had covered with gods, the greatest night's work any hands ever did. The tellings do not agree on whether he grieved or simply picked up his chisel again — and there is a whole life's lesson in not being sure which.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-unakoti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.unakoti|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from One Less Than a Crore","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.twipra-name-1":{"src":"story:fk.twipra-name:scenes:1:text","title":"The Land Beside the Water · 2 of 8","body":"Her grandmother answered in Kokborok, because Kokborok is her language — the tongue of the Borok people, the Tripuris. \"Listen to the name itself, child. Twi is water. Every river in these hills carries it — say the river names and you are saying twi, twi, twi. Twipra: the land beside the waters. That is us. We even named ourselves for speech and people: kok is speech, Borok are the people.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-twipra-name.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.twipra-name|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Land Beside the Water","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.twipra-name-2":{"src":"story:fk.twipra-name:scenes:2:text","title":"The Land Beside the Water · 3 of 8","body":"Her grandfather cleared his throat, which in every language means \"there is another answer.\" \"The kings' chronicle says otherwise.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-twipra-name.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.twipra-name|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Land Beside the Water","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.twipra-name-3":{"src":"story:fk.twipra-name:scenes:3:text","title":"The Land Beside the Water · 4 of 8","body":"The girl looked from one to the other. Water and people on one side; kings and a goddess on the other. And the phone line to her cousin waiting.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-twipra-name.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.twipra-name|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Land Beside the Water","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.twipra-name-5":{"src":"story:fk.twipra-name:scenes:5:text","title":"The Land Beside the Water · 6 of 8","body":"And that is honestly where the matter stands, and scholars have argued it happily for a very long time. Places that many peoples have loved tend to end up with more than one name-story, one from each direction of loving.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-twipra-name.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.twipra-name|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Land Beside the Water","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.twipra-name-6":{"src":"story:fk.twipra-name:scenes:6:text","title":"The Land Beside the Water · 7 of 8","body":"The cousin abroad, for the record, liked the water answer best, and the grandfather pretended not to mind, and the grandmother did not pretend anything, because she had heard the verdict perfectly well and considered it correct. Twi, she said again, to nobody in particular. Water.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-twipra-name.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.twipra-name|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Land Beside the Water","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.kharchi-fourteen-1":{"src":"story:fk.kharchi-fourteen:scenes:1:text","title":"The Week of the Fourteen Gods · 2 of 8","body":"Their keepers are the Chantai — priests from the Tripuri community, whose office has come down from the old royal days, long before the temple had a road to it. The kings of the Manikya line, who ruled Tripura for centuries, kept this puja as the kingdom's own; the kingdom has become a state, and the puja has simply carried on.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kharchi-fourteen.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kharchi-fourteen|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Week of the Fourteen Gods","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kharchi-fourteen-2":{"src":"story:fk.kharchi-fourteen:scenes:2:text","title":"The Week of the Fourteen Gods · 3 of 8","body":"And here is the detail to keep: the fourteen have two names each. A Kokborok name, from the hills — the elders speak of the eldest as Subrai Raja — and a Sanskrit name from the plains tradition. Two languages, praying at one altar, for as long as anyone can measure.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kharchi-fourteen.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kharchi-fourteen|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Week of the Fourteen Gods","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kharchi-fourteen-3":{"src":"story:fk.kharchi-fourteen:scenes:3:text","title":"The Week of the Fourteen Gods · 4 of 8","body":"Every July comes their week: Kharchi Puja. The fourteen are brought out and carried in procession to the river, and there they are bathed — carefully, with honour, the way you would bathe a beloved grandparent — and carried home again shining.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kharchi-fourteen.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kharchi-fourteen|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Week of the Fourteen Gods","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kharchi-fourteen-5":{"src":"story:fk.kharchi-fourteen:scenes:5:text","title":"The Week of the Fourteen Gods · 6 of 8","body":"And around the temple, for the whole week, a mela — one of the great fairs of the state. Hill families and plains families, Kokborok and Bengali, stalls and wheels and sugar-smoke, everybody in everybody's way in the friendliest possible manner. The fourteen sit at the centre of it like grandparents at a wedding.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kharchi-fourteen.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kharchi-fourteen|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Week of the Fourteen Gods","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kharchi-fourteen-6":{"src":"story:fk.kharchi-fourteen:scenes:6:text","title":"The Week of the Fourteen Gods · 7 of 8","body":"Once it was the king's own puja, paid from the palace. Today the state itself honours the week, and the crowds are larger than any king ever saw. Guardians, it turns out, outlast the thrones that first bowed to them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kharchi-fourteen.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kharchi-fourteen|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Week of the Fourteen Gods","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.garia-puja-1":{"src":"story:fk.garia-puja:scenes:1:text","title":"The God Who Comes Up Your Steps · 2 of 8","body":"He is the giver of the year's essentials — the harvest, the herds, the children, the luck of the house — and for his days he takes a humble and beautiful form: a bamboo staff, dressed and honoured, carried by his own procession. In these hills bamboo builds the house, fences the field and carries the water. Of course the giver of plenty is at home in it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-garia-puja.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.garia-puja|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The God Who Comes Up Your Steps","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.garia-puja-2":{"src":"story:fk.garia-puja:scenes:2:text","title":"The God Who Comes Up Your Steps · 3 of 8","body":"The procession goes from courtyard to courtyard — an aangan at a time, no house too small — with drums and flute and the Garia dancers, who dance in every yard they enter. The blessing is danced into each household separately. Nobody has to travel to be included; being included travels.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-garia-puja.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.garia-puja|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The God Who Comes Up Your Steps","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.garia-puja-3":{"src":"story:fk.garia-puja:scenes:3:text","title":"The God Who Comes Up Your Steps · 4 of 8","body":"Each family welcomes him with what the kitchen and the field can give — rice, fruit, an egg, cotton — simple things, the things his year actually provides, offered back to him at the start of it. The circle could not be drawn plainer: what he gives is what he is given.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-garia-puja.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.garia-puja|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The God Who Comes Up Your Steps","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.garia-puja-5":{"src":"story:fk.garia-puja:scenes:5:text","title":"The God Who Comes Up Your Steps · 6 of 8","body":"The drumming, once heard, stays with you — it comes up the lane before the procession does, so that the blessing sends its sound ahead like a runner, and children spill out of doorways to wait. Being waited for at every single house: that is a god's week well spent.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-garia-puja.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.garia-puja|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The God Who Comes Up Your Steps","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.garia-puja-6":{"src":"story:fk.garia-puja:scenes:6:text","title":"The God Who Comes Up Your Steps · 7 of 8","body":"And when the dancers have gone on to the next courtyard and the drums have faded down the lane, each house is left with the same quiet arithmetic: a little rice given, a whole year asked for. Garia has never been reported to object to the exchange rate.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-garia-puja.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.garia-puja|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The God Who Comes Up Your Steps","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.rignai-loom-1":{"src":"story:fk.rignai-loom:scenes:1:text","title":"The Loom Tied to the Weaver · 2 of 8","body":"This is the loin-loom, the backstrap loom, and the hill peoples of the whole Northeast are its masters. In Tripura it is the women's art of the Borok communities — Tripuri, Reang, Jamatia and their neighbours — passed from mother to daughter for as long as there have been mothers and daughters.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-rignai-loom.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.rignai-loom|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Loom Tied to the Weaver","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.rignai-loom-2":{"src":"story:fk.rignai-loom:scenes:2:text","title":"The Loom Tied to the Weaver · 3 of 8","body":"What comes off it, thread by thread, is the rignai — the woven wrap the women of these hills wear — and the risha, the woven breast-cloth and stole. Stripes and diamonds and borders in colours that carry across a valley: reds, blacks, oranges, the occasional green that means business.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-rignai-loom.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.rignai-loom|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Loom Tied to the Weaver","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.rignai-loom-3":{"src":"story:fk.rignai-loom:scenes:3:text","title":"The Loom Tied to the Weaver · 4 of 8","body":"And the patterns are not decoration. They are belonging. Each community weaves its own rignai — its own colours, its own arrangements — so that a woman who knows cloth can stand in a market and read where every other woman is from, the way you read a name. The pattern is an address written in thread.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-rignai-loom.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.rignai-loom|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Loom Tied to the Weaver","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.rignai-loom-5":{"src":"story:fk.rignai-loom:scenes:5:text","title":"The Loom Tied to the Weaver · 6 of 8","body":"A risha is more than clothing, too — laid across a guest's shoulders it is an honour, given at life's big doorways it is a blessing you can hold. When Tripura wants to honour somebody, it very often does it in cloth, and the cloth was woven by somebody's mother on a loom tied to her own body.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-rignai-loom.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.rignai-loom|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Loom Tied to the Weaver","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.rignai-loom-6":{"src":"story:fk.rignai-loom:scenes:6:text","title":"The Loom Tied to the Weaver · 7 of 8","body":"And because the loom folds up into almost nothing — a bundle of sticks and thread you can hang on a wall — it has travelled everywhere the weavers have. No workshop required, no machine to lose. A woman with a strap and a wall can set up five hundred years of tradition on any veranda on earth.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-rignai-loom.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.rignai-loom|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Loom Tied to the Weaver","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.matabari-turtles-1":{"src":"story:fk.matabari-turtles:scenes:1:text","title":"The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake · 2 of 8","body":"It is counted among the Shakti Pithas — the great seats of the Goddess spread across the whole map of this land, each one a place where the Mother is held to be specially present. Tripura's seat is one of the honoured ones, and pilgrims come from far beyond the state.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-matabari-turtles.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.matabari-turtles|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.matabari-turtles-2":{"src":"story:fk.matabari-turtles:scenes:2:text","title":"The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake · 3 of 8","body":"And the hill it stands on has a shape people love to point out: low, rounded, humped like a tortoise's back rising from the paddy. Kurma Pitha, the tortoise seat, they call it — remember that, because the tortoise theme is about to become extremely real.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-matabari-turtles.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.matabari-turtles|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.matabari-turtles-3":{"src":"story:fk.matabari-turtles:scenes:3:text","title":"The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake · 4 of 8","body":"Beside the temple lies Kalyan Sagar — the lake of wellbeing — and in it live turtles. Big ones. Old ones. Turtles that surface beside the ghat steps with the unhurried confidence of creatures who have never once been troubled, because they never once have: no net has ever been cast in that lake, no hook, not ever.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-matabari-turtles.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.matabari-turtles|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.matabari-turtles-5":{"src":"story:fk.matabari-turtles:scenes:5:text","title":"The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake · 6 of 8","body":"Then there is the pera — the milk sweet made and offered at Matabari, famous across the state; prasad here has its own taste that people carry home wrapped in leaf and paper, and a little of the Mother's house travels with every parcel.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-matabari-turtles.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.matabari-turtles|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.matabari-turtles-6":{"src":"story:fk.matabari-turtles:scenes:6:text","title":"The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake · 7 of 8","body":"At Diwali the temple holds one of the great fairs of Tripura — lamps everywhere, the courtyard full, the lake throwing every flame back double. Hill families and plains families side by side at the same threshold, which is what a mother's house is for.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-matabari-turtles.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.matabari-turtles|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother's House by the Turtle Lake","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bizhu-1":{"src":"story:fk.bizhu:scenes:1:text","title":"Flowers on the Water at Dawn · 2 of 8","body":"It opens with the loveliest job in the festival calendar, and it belongs to the children. Before dawn on Phool Bizhu — the flower day — they are up in the half-dark, gathering flowers by the armful. Down to the river or the pond, and the flowers go onto the water with the day's first light — set afloat as a greeting, the old year thanked and sent off garlanded.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bizhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bizhu|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Flowers on the Water at Dawn","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bizhu-2":{"src":"story:fk.bizhu:scenes:2:text","title":"Flowers on the Water at Dawn · 3 of 8","body":"Flowers go on the doorways too, and on the house shrine, and behind ears wherever there are ears. A house dressed in flowers by its own children, before breakfast: that is how a Chakma year begins.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bizhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bizhu|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Flowers on the Water at Dawn","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bizhu-3":{"src":"story:fk.bizhu:scenes:3:text","title":"Flowers on the Water at Dawn · 4 of 8","body":"The great day is Mul Bizhu, and its centrepiece is a dish called pajon — a slow pot into which goes every vegetable the season can offer. Not three kinds. Not five. Families take pride in the count the way other cooks take pride in secrecy; the more the year has given, the more goes in the pot.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bizhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bizhu|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Flowers on the Water at Dawn","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bizhu-5":{"src":"story:fk.bizhu:scenes:5:text","title":"Flowers on the Water at Dawn · 6 of 8","body":"The elders are at the centre of it all — blessings collected, respects paid, the old bathed in honour by the young in many families. And after the great day comes a quieter third: a gentle day, for rest and blessings, easing the festival closed instead of slamming it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bizhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bizhu|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Flowers on the Water at Dawn","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bizhu-6":{"src":"story:fk.bizhu:scenes:6:text","title":"Flowers on the Water at Dawn · 7 of 8","body":"Now finish the pattern you have been collecting: Bihu in Assam, Bwisagu among the Bodos, Garia's days in the Tripura hills — and Bizhu among the Chakma, all in the same April turning, all cousins of one great festival family that stretches from these hills across borders far beyond. One spring, many names, and every name is a people greeting the year in their own voice.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bizhu.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bizhu|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Flowers on the Water at Dawn","facts":[["From","The Eastern Hills"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-sarabhai-1":{"src":"story:sci-sarabhai:scenes:1:text","title":"The Rocket in the Church · 2 of 6","body":"In 1962 India asked him to start something that sounded, frankly, absurd: a space programme, in a country where most villages had no electricity. Plenty of sensible people said so out loud.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rocket in the Church","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-sarabhai-2":{"src":"story:sci-sarabhai:scenes:2:text","title":"The Rocket in the Church · 3 of 6","body":"For the first launch site his scientists needed a spot close to the magnetic equator, where the upper air is at its most interesting. The map pointed at Thumba — a quiet fishing village near Thiruvananthapuram in Kerala. And in the middle of the chosen ground stood the village's church, St Mary Magdalene's.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rocket in the Church","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-sarabhai-4":{"src":"story:sci-sarabhai:scenes:4:text","title":"The Rocket in the Church · 5 of 6","body":"From that church grew ISRO, the Indian Space Research Organisation — the organisation that went on to put satellites over every monsoon, television into village schools exactly as promised, and, in time, spacecraft around the Moon and Mars. Sarabhai did not live to see most of it; he died in 1971, at just fifty-two. The programme he planted kept his promise for him.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rocket in the Church","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-kalam-1":{"src":"story:sci-kalam:scenes:1:text","title":"The Newspaper Boy of Rameswaram · 2 of 6","body":"Then the Second World War reached even Rameswaram's railway line: the train stopped halting at the station, and the town's newspapers had to be flung out in bundles from the moving carriage. His cousin Samsuddin, who distributed the papers, needed a boy quick enough to catch them. So every dawn, Abdul caught the news out of the air, ran his delivery rounds, and then went to school.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-kalam.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-kalam|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Newspaper Boy of Rameswaram","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-kalam-2":{"src":"story:sci-kalam:scenes:2:text","title":"The Newspaper Boy of Rameswaram · 3 of 6","body":"He told, all his life, of a schoolteacher in Rameswaram who one day taught the class how birds fly — drawing the bird on the board, the wing, the lift, the tail steering — and, when the class did not follow, took the boys to the seashore in the evening to watch the seabirds do it. Kalam said that was the day his life found its subject.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-kalam.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-kalam|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Newspaper Boy of Rameswaram","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-kalam-4":{"src":"story:sci-kalam:scenes:4:text","title":"The Newspaper Boy of Rameswaram · 5 of 6","body":"Missiles and rockets made him famous; children made him beloved. In 2002 the newspaper boy of Rameswaram was elected President of India — and the morning papers, the kind he had once caught out of the air at a moving train, now carried his photograph on the front page.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-kalam.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-kalam|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Newspaper Boy of Rameswaram","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-tessy-1":{"src":"story:sci-tessy:scenes:1:text","title":"The Engineer Who Steered the Fire · 2 of 6","body":"She studied engineering, and in 1988 joined DRDO — the organisation that builds India's defence technology — where she was put to work on the Agni programme, the long-range missiles named for fire, in the years when A. P. J. Abdul Kalam led those corridors. Her specialism was the deep end of the field: guidance. Lighting a rocket is the loud part.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-tessy.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-tessy|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Engineer Who Steered the Fire","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-tessy-2":{"src":"story:sci-tessy:scenes:2:text","title":"The Engineer Who Steered the Fire · 3 of 6","body":"She spent twenty years mastering that art — guidance, trajectory, the fierce physics of re-entry, when a returning craft must survive its own meteor-hot plunge back through the air. She rose the way engineers respect most: flight by flight, problem by solved problem. Missile teams keep no polite fictions; the bird flies or it does not, and everyone knows whose work is aboard.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-tessy.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-tessy|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Engineer Who Steered the Fire","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-tessy-4":{"src":"story:sci-tessy:scenes:4:text","title":"The Engineer Who Steered the Fire · 5 of 6","body":"The work carried her on: she went on to head all of DRDO's aeronautical systems — thousands of engineers, everything that flies.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-tessy.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-tessy|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Engineer Who Steered the Fire","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"Duryodhana got there first and took the chair at the head of the bed, because he was a king and that is where a king sits. Arjuna came in after and sat down on the floor at the foot, and waited.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"Krishna woke up and saw Arjuna first, because that is who was in front of him.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"\"You are both welcome,\" he said, \"and you have both come for the same thing, and I am related to both of you. So here is what I have. On one side, my whole army — every soldier I have. On the other side, me. Alone. And I will not pick up a weapon or fight for one minute of this war.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"\"I saw Arjuna first, and he is the younger. He chooses first. Argue with me about it later.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-6":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"\"You,\" said Arjuna. \"I will take you.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"Duryodhana was out of the door before anybody could change their mind, absolutely delighted, and honestly you cannot blame him. He had come for soldiers and he had got all of them, and it had cost him nothing.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"\"You have just chosen a man who will not lift a weapon,\" said Krishna, when they were alone. \"You could have had the army. Are you sure?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-22-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":22},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:21:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Both Cousins Come Asking","body":"\"I did not choose a weapon,\" said Arjuna. \"I chose you. I want you where I can hear you.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","22 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"He stood up in that hall — the same hall — and put the case in front of them plainly. Thirteen years had been served, exactly as agreed, down to the last day. The kingdom was owed back. It was not a favour. It was the terms.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"Duryodhana said no.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"So Krishna came down. Not the kingdom, then. Half of it. No. A province, then. No. And in the end he stood in the middle of the greatest court in the world and asked, for the sake of peace, for five villages. One for each brother. That was the final offer.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"\"Bhishma begged him,\" said one of the courtiers who was in the room. \"Drona begged him. His own father asked him. Vidura told him what would happen to us all. And Karna, who never once told him what he wanted to hear, told him to take it.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-6":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"And he was not simply being greedy, and this is the part that matters. He believed that kingdom was his. He had believed it since he was small. He had been told so by his uncle every day for thirty years, and never once made to sit still and hear anything else. By the time he says that sentence, he cannot hear it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"Then somebody in the hall gave an order to seize the messenger, which is about as low as a court can go, and there was a moment of real horror in that room — and Krishna simply walked out through all of it and got into his chariot.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"\"I have done what I came to do,\" he said at the gate. \"Nobody will be able to say afterwards that there was no other way. There was. It was five villages. It was in this room this morning and every one of you watched it go.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-23-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|23","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":23},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:22:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Five Villages","body":"On the way out of the city he stopped, and asked for someone to be brought to him privately. And the man who came out to the chariot was Karna.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 23","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","23 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"The night before, the elders from both camps met in the middle and wrote down the rules, together, in daylight, agreed by everybody.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"No fighting after sunset. One against one, and never more. Nobody who has lost his chariot. Nobody who has put his weapon down. Nobody who is running, nobody asleep, nobody who is not a soldier. Charioteers, animals and camp people are not touched.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"They meant it when they wrote it. Keep the list in your head. Almost every single line of it gets broken in the next eighteen days, mostly by the side the poem is following.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"In the morning Arjuna's chariot went out with Krishna holding the reins, and on the flag above it sat an old monkey who had promised, years ago, on a forest path, to be there.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"\"I said I would come,\" said Hanuman, settling himself. \"Nobody down there is going to enjoy the noise I make.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"His brothers thought he had lost his nerve. His own soldiers thought he was surrendering. He walked the whole way in silence and went to Bhishma and put his head on the old man's feet.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"\"I am going to fight you,\" he said. \"Give me your permission.\" And Bhishma, who had taught him to walk, put a hand on his head and said, \"If you had not come, I would have been ashamed of you. Fight. I am tied to that throne, not to the boy sitting on it. Ask me later how to beat me, and I will tell you.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"He did the same at Drona. The same at Kripa. Every one of them gave him their blessing and told him to come back and ask again when he needed the answer, and then went and stood in the line opposite him.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-25-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|25","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":25},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:24:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Night Before","body":"Then the conches went, all along both lines, and eighteen armies stood up. And Arjuna leaned forward and said, \"Take me out into the middle. I want to see who I have come to fight.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 25","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","25 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"And Abhimanyu, who was sixteen years old, and who was Arjuna's son, said he could get in.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"The story goes that he had learned it before he was born, listening from inside his mother while his father explained it — and that she had fallen asleep before the part about coming out again. So he had half of it. He had always had exactly half of it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"\"I can open it,\" he said. \"I cannot close it behind me. Come in with me and stay close and we are all fine.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"He went in like a stone through water, and the wheel opened, and his uncles came in behind him — and then the door shut. One man held that gap and none of the others could get through it, and the boy was inside, on his own, with the whole army.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"They could not beat him fairly, so they stopped trying. Six of the greatest warriors alive came at one sixteen-year-old at the same time, which was against the first rule on the list they had all written together in daylight ten days before.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"The poem does not make any of this exciting. It slows down and it goes quiet and it makes you watch, and then it stops, and that is the whole point of the page.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"Arjuna came back at evening, and the camp was too quiet, and everybody found somewhere else to look. He had to ask three times before anybody would tell him.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"What he did next is the moment this war stops being a war and becomes something else. He swore that he would kill the man who had held that gap before the sun went down the next day, and that if he failed he would walk into fire himself.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-28-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|28","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":28},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:27:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Boy Inside the Wheel","body":"And after that the rules on the list came off, one at a time, on both sides, and this is where every reader has to notice that it is our side taking them off.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 28","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","28 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"He walked away from the field with his mace and went into a lake and lay down at the bottom of it, in the cold water, and stayed there. Not hiding, he said afterwards. Resting. There is nobody left to fight beside, he said, so what exactly am I hurrying for.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"They found him. They stood on the bank and called him out, and Bhima said things that were designed to work, and after a while the water broke and he came up with the mace on his shoulder.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"\"One at a time,\" he said. \"Any of you. Pick one and send him and I will fight him and then the next, and if I am still standing, the kingdom is mine and you can all go home.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"Bhima went. And they were very evenly matched, more evenly than anybody had expected, and it went on and on until it was clear that Bhima was not going to win it fairly.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"So somebody on the bank tapped his own thigh, where everybody could see it, and Bhima understood, and struck below the waist, which was against the rules of that particular fight and every man watching knew it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"And Duryodhana lay on the ground and made the last speech, and the poem gives it to him straight, with nobody correcting him at the end of it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"\"I ruled the whole earth. I had friends who stayed. I gave away what I wanted to give away and I never once asked anybody to make me smaller. I am dying on a field with my brothers, not in a bed. Now tell me — what exactly have any of you won?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"Nobody answered. And then, in the night, while the winning camp was asleep, something happened that took from them nearly everything they had left. It was done to sleeping people. The book that tells it is called the Book of the Sleeping, and there is nothing anywhere in it that anybody would call a victory.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-31-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|31","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":31},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:30:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Last Day","body":"Draupadi lost all five of her sons in that one night, after all of it, after thirteen years, at the end. And when the man who had done it was caught and brought in front of her, she asked for him to be let go, because his mother was alive, and she had just found out what that was.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 31","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","31 of 33"]]}});
